understand that he's also a practitioner.

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:35.300
So thank you, Andrew Galpin.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:40.300
Andy Galpin for your
support in these episodes

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:44.290
and we hope to have you as
a guest on the podcast soon.

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:46.210
So nerve muscle, blood, heart and lungs.

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:48.470
Let's talk about neurons
and how they work.

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:49.303
Okay?

00:22:49.303 --> 00:22:51.160
But I want to tell you about an experiment

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:53.880
that's going to make it very clear

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:58.340
why quitting is a mental
thing not a physical thing.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:00.010
So why do we quit?

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:02.590
Well, an experiment was
done a couple years ago

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:04.160
and was published in the journal Cell.

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:06.480
Cell Press journal, excellent journal

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:11.480
showing that there is a class
of neurons in our brain stem,

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:16.540
in the back of our brain that
if they shut off, we quit.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:24.000
Now, these neurons release epinephrin.

00:23:24.030 --> 00:23:25.250
Epinephrin is adrenaline.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:28.230
And anytime we are engaged
in effort of any kind,

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:29.730
we are releasing epinephrin.

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.960
Anytime we're awake really,
we are releasing epinephrin

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:33.893
into our brain.

00:23:35.210 --> 00:23:38.740
In fact, this little roup of neurons

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:40.930
in the back of our brain is
called the locus coeruleus

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:41.980
if you like.

00:23:41.980 --> 00:23:44.260
It's churning out epinephrin all the time,

00:23:44.260 --> 00:23:45.970
but if something stresses us out,

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:47.880
it churns out more and then it acts

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.720
as kind of an alertness
signal for the whole brain.

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:52.830
We also of course have
adrenaline epinephrin

00:23:52.830 --> 00:23:55.690
released in our body which
makes our body ready for things.

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:58.650
So think about epinephrin
as a readiness signal.

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:01.060
And when we are engaged in effort,

00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:03.310
this readiness signal is
being churned into our brain.

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:05.030
When we relax and we're falling asleep,

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:06.963
epinephrin levels are low.

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:11.870
Okay, so they did a really
interesting experiment

00:24:11.870 --> 00:24:16.870
where they had subjects
engage in bouts of effort

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:20.220
of trying to move forward toward a goal.

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.550
But they manipulated
the visual environment

00:24:22.550 --> 00:24:23.520
with the stripes,

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.993
kind of like fences passing
on both sides of them.

00:24:27.220 --> 00:24:30.340
And by doing that, they
could trick subjects

00:24:30.340 --> 00:24:33.990
into thinking that their
effort was either allowing them

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:35.460
to move forward, right?

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:39.200
Because these rungs on the
fence were moving past,

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.880
or that their effort was futile

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:43.460
that they were no longer moving forward

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:45.310
because they would make
the runs move slowly

00:24:45.310 --> 00:24:47.730
even though the subjects
were making a lot of effort

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:48.950
to move forward.

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:49.783
Okay?

00:24:49.783 --> 00:24:52.400
So this is analogous or
similar to being on a treadmill

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.240
and you're trying to
walk on this treadmill

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:57.320
and you just can't move
the conveyor, right?

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:59.010
Or you're in virtual reality

00:24:59.010 --> 00:25:00.470
and you're putting a ton of effort,

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:02.670
but it seems like you're
moving excruciatingly slow.

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:05.290
I had this experience
recently in real life.

00:25:05.290 --> 00:25:07.450
I was doing a swim in the Pacific,

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:10.660
I was trying to go
south and I was swimming

00:25:10.660 --> 00:25:12.080
and I was caught in a current,

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.200
not the cup that pulls you out to ocean.

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:16.370
And I kept looking to my left,

00:25:16.370 --> 00:25:19.100
and I saw this hotel on the shoreline,

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:20.860
and then I was swimming and swimming

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:21.780
and swimming and swimming,

00:25:21.780 --> 00:25:24.250
and 20 minutes later I looked
to my left and the hotel

00:25:24.250 --> 00:25:26.190
is still exactly where it was before,

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:28.440
which meant that I wasn't moving.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:29.573
It felt futile.

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:34.350
Eventually, either the current
changed or something changed

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:37.670
and I eventually swam past the hotel,

00:25:37.670 --> 00:25:40.213
got back on the beach and
eventually drove home.

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.180
That's essentially what
they did in this experiment.

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:45.500
But what they found was these neurons

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:46.993
that release epinephrin,

00:25:48.440 --> 00:25:51.340
there's another cell type called glia,

00:25:51.340 --> 00:25:53.310
which actually means glue in Latin,

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:55.510
that is paying attention
to how much epinephrin

00:25:55.510 --> 00:25:56.343
is being released.

00:25:56.343 --> 00:26:00.090
And at some point the
system reaches a threshold.

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:01.380
It reaches this threshold

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:04.410
and it shuts off the
release of more epinephrin.

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:06.170
It's like I quit, that's it,

00:26:06.170 --> 00:26:07.683
no more effort signal.

00:26:09.240 --> 00:26:12.160
If they could extend the time

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:14.973
before those glia said, ah, enough.

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:18.340
If they could release more
adrenaline into the system,

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:20.670
then subjects would keep going.

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:25.670
So our desire to continue
or put differently,

00:26:25.690 --> 00:26:29.490
our willingness to continue
and our desire to quit

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:33.470
is mediated by events
between our two ears.

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:35.580
Now, that doesn't mean that
the body's not involved,

00:26:35.580 --> 00:26:37.710
but it means that neurons
are critically important.

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:39.950
So we have two categories of
neurons that are important.

00:26:39.950 --> 00:26:42.040
The ones in our head that tell us get up

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:43.860
and go out and take that run.

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:47.840
And the ones that allow us
and encourage us to continue

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:48.673
that run,

00:26:48.673 --> 00:26:50.200
and we have neurons that shut things off

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:51.400
and say no more.

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:52.950
And we of course have the neurons

00:26:52.950 --> 00:26:55.670
that connect to our muscles
and control our muscles.

00:26:55.670 --> 00:26:59.640
But the reason we quit is
rarely because our body quits,

00:26:59.640 --> 00:27:01.540
our mind quits.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:02.810
Now, we never want to encourage people

00:27:02.810 --> 00:27:05.960
to drive themselves to
the point of injury,

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:09.460
that's not going to be good for anybody,

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:13.150
but it is good to know that it's neural.

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:15.060
Our ability to persist is neural.

00:27:15.060 --> 00:27:17.530
So when people say is it, I hear that,

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:19.970
you know, sports or effort or fighting,

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:24.003
or it's 90% mental, 10% physical.

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:27.230
That whole discussion
about how much is mental,

00:27:27.230 --> 00:27:28.063
how much is physical?

00:27:28.063 --> 00:27:29.320
Is absolutely silly.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:31.670
It just proves that there's no knowledge

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:34.390
of the underlying biology
behind that statement.

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:36.680
It's neither mental nor physical.

00:27:36.680 --> 00:27:38.720
Everything is physical,

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:40.050
everything is neurons.

00:27:40.050 --> 00:27:43.330
Your thinking is the
responsibility of chemicals

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:44.830
and electrical signals in your head.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:47.910
So it's not 90% mental, 10% physical.

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:49.110
It's not 50/50.

00:27:49.110 --> 00:27:53.900
It's not 70/30, it's 100% nervous system.

00:27:53.900 --> 00:27:55.410
It's neurons.

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:56.243
Okay?

00:27:56.243 --> 00:27:58.240
So when people say mental or physical,

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:01.090
understand it's 100% neural.

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:03.814
And I'd love for the,
how much of it is mental

00:28:03.814 --> 00:28:06.593
and how much is physical
to just disappear.

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:08.720
That argument means nothing

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:10.070
and it's not actionable.

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:13.840
Now, what do nerves need in
order to continue to fire?

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:16.650
What do you need in order
to get neurons to say,

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:17.890
I will persist.

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:19.880
Well, they need glucose.

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:24.110
Unless you're on keto
and ketogenic adapted,

00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:26.400
you need carbohydrate is glucose.

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:28.890
That's what neurons run on.

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:31.730
And you need electrolytes.

00:28:31.730 --> 00:28:35.050
Neurons have what's called
a sodium potassium pump

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:35.883
blah, blah, blah.

00:28:35.883 --> 00:28:36.840
They generate electricity.

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:37.820
We could go into all this.

00:28:37.820 --> 00:28:39.090
I will probably do an entire lecture

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:40.070
about the action potential,

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:43.930
but basically in order to
get nerve cells to fire,

00:28:43.930 --> 00:28:45.260
to contract muscle,

00:28:45.260 --> 00:28:46.900
to say, I'm going to continue.

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:49.970
You need sufficient sodium, salt,

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:54.510
because the action potential
the actual firing of neurons

00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:57.320
is driven by sodium entering the cell,

00:28:57.320 --> 00:28:58.940
rushing into the cell.

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:01.930
And then there's a removal of potassium.

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:04.370
And then there's a kind of
resetting of those levels

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:06.090
by something called the
sodium potassium pump.

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.014
And the sodium potassium pump and sodium

00:29:08.014 --> 00:29:09.890
and action potentials,

00:29:09.890 --> 00:29:13.580
even if you don't know anything
about that is ATP dependent.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:15.450
It requires energy.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:18.573
So you need energy in order
to get neurons to fire.

00:29:19.510 --> 00:29:21.270
And it is pH dependent.

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:23.560
It depends on the conditions

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:26.500
or the environment within the
brain being of a certain pH

00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:27.580
or acidity.

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:32.050
pH is about how acid or how
basic the environment is?

00:29:32.050 --> 00:29:34.280
And we will talk a little
bit about pH in simple terms

00:29:34.280 --> 00:29:35.113
so you can understand.

00:29:35.113 --> 00:29:37.820
So nerves need salt, they need potassium.

00:29:37.820 --> 00:29:39.720
And it turns out they need magnesium

00:29:39.720 --> 00:29:42.120
and you need glucose and carbohydrates

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:43.410
in order to power those neurons,

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:46.400
unless you are running on ketones.

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:47.860
And to run on ketones,

00:29:47.860 --> 00:29:50.950
you have to make sure that
you're fully keto adapted.

00:29:50.950 --> 00:29:54.560
I will talk about adding in
ketones on top of carbohydrate

00:29:54.560 --> 00:29:56.340
at the end of the episode.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:57.980
Okay, so that's how nerves work.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:29:59.030
You need carbohydrate,

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:00.920
you need sodium, potassium and magnesium

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:03.263
in order to drive the brain.

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:05.063
Muscle.

00:30:05.063 --> 00:30:08.830
Muscle is going to engage
in generating energy first

00:30:08.830 --> 00:30:11.510
by using this phosphocreatine system.

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:14.080
High bouts of effort
really intense effort,

00:30:14.080 --> 00:30:16.510
short-lived seconds to minutes,

00:30:16.510 --> 00:30:17.700
but probably more like seconds

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:20.120
is going to be this phosphate
creating literally a fuel source

00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:21.730
in the muscle that you're going to burn,

00:30:21.730 --> 00:30:24.830
just like you would logs on a fire.

00:30:24.830 --> 00:30:28.320
And glycogen which is stored
carbohydrate in the muscle,

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:30.730
that also can be burned
just like logs on a fire

00:30:30.730 --> 00:30:31.700
to generate energy.

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:33.280
So let me make this crystal clear.

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:35.200
If you move your wrist
towards your shoulder

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:37.330
and contract your bicep really hard,

00:30:37.330 --> 00:30:39.980
muscle fibers are burning
up their own carbohydrate.

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:41.800
They're converting that into ATP

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:44.040
in order to generate that energy.

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:45.030
Okay?

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:50.030
And pH is important and
temperature is important.

00:30:50.510 --> 00:30:53.820
In the episode on supercharge
your physical performance,

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:57.780
I talked all about how by
using cooling specifically

00:30:57.780 --> 00:30:59.730
of the palms or the bottoms of the feet

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:02.700
or the cheeks of the face
using particular methods,

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:07.700
you can adjust the temperature
of the body and of muscle

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:10.130
in a way that allows you to do more work,

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:12.470
to do more reps, to run further,

00:31:12.470 --> 00:31:14.780
to keep going and to persist.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:19.780
And that's because if temperature
is too low or too high,

00:31:20.150 --> 00:31:24.010
then ATP is not going to be available

00:31:24.010 --> 00:31:24.843
because of this whole thing

00:31:24.843 --> 00:31:26.700
called the pyruvate kinase pathway

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:28.680
and the temperature
dependence of pyruvate kinase.

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:30.520
Check out that episode if you
want to learn more about that.

00:31:30.520 --> 00:31:33.600
But temperature is important
and pH is also important.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:34.720
So we got nerve, muscle,

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:36.550
and then there's stuff in our blood

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:38.850
that's available as an energy source.

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:40.410
And in blood, we got glucose.

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:42.240
So literally blood sugar
that's floating around.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:44.470
So let's say you have
fasted for three days,

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:46.480
your blood glucose is
going to be very low.

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:48.820
So that's not going to
be a great fuel source,

00:31:48.820 --> 00:31:50.720
but you will start to liberate fats

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:53.350
from your adipose tissue from your fat.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:55.810
Fatty acids will start to
mobilize into the bloodstream

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:58.900
and you can burn those for energy.

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:01.680
And oxygen in your blood when
you inhale, [inhales deeply]

00:32:01.680 --> 00:32:03.190
you're bringing oxygen into your blood.

00:32:03.190 --> 00:32:05.410
So these are all fuel
sources in your neurons,

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:07.330
in your muscle, in your blood,

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:12.330
in your various tissues that
are providing the opportunity

00:32:14.825 --> 00:32:17.530
to induce effort whether
or not it's a run or swim

00:32:17.530 --> 00:32:19.290
or writing or talking.

00:32:19.290 --> 00:32:22.890
Now, there are some other
factors that are important

00:32:22.890 --> 00:32:26.130
and those are the heart
which is going to move blood.

00:32:26.130 --> 00:32:30.030
So the more that the heart
can move blood and oxygen,

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:34.160
well, the more fuel that's
going to be available for you

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:38.230
to engage in muscular
effort and thinking effort.

00:32:38.230 --> 00:32:41.670
So your heart vitally important
to your muscles ability

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:43.660
to work and your brain's ability to work.

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:46.690
And as I've mentioned, oxygen a few times,

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:49.040
it should be obvious then that
the lungs are very important.

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:50.710
You need to bring oxygen in,

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:52.700
and distribute it to all these tissues,

00:32:52.700 --> 00:32:55.321
because oxygen is critical
for the conversion

00:32:55.321 --> 00:33:00.130
of carbohydrates and
the conversion of fats.

00:33:00.130 --> 00:33:02.620
And we could get into the discussion about

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:05.540
whether or not oxygen is important
for ketogenic metabolism,

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:06.960
but you need oxygen there,

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:09.690
you need to breathe and you
need to breathe properly.

00:33:09.690 --> 00:33:14.330
So I just covered what would
normally be about four lectures

00:33:14.330 --> 00:33:16.990
of energy consumption
and energy utilization.

00:33:16.990 --> 00:33:18.350
I didn't go into much detail at all.

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:21.470
But what I want you to imagine is that,

00:33:21.470 --> 00:33:23.420
you've got these different cell types.

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:25.620
You've got neurons, you've got muscle,

00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:28.480
they need to collaborate
in order to generate effort

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:30.210
or to make the decision to do something

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:33.110
or to think hard or to
run hard or to run far.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:37.290
And then you've got fuel
sources both in the neurons,

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:40.290
in the muscle, in your blood,

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:42.990
and then the heart and
lungs are going to help

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:45.763
distribute the oxygen and those fuels.

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:48.570
And of course you have
that little energy pack

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:52.140
that we call the liver that
will allow you to pull out

00:33:52.140 --> 00:33:55.340
a little more carbohydrate
if you need it for work.

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:56.173
Okay?

00:33:56.173 --> 00:33:59.210
So that's as much as I want to
cover about energy consumption,

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:00.900
because that's a lot.

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:03.060
But what it tells you is that when you eat

00:34:03.060 --> 00:34:05.050
and you use food as a fuel source,

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:07.890
that food can be broken down

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:10.070
and you can immediately burn the glucose

00:34:10.070 --> 00:34:10.940
that's in your bloodstream,

00:34:10.940 --> 00:34:13.780
or you can rely on some of
the stored fuel in your liver,

00:34:13.780 --> 00:34:16.010
or you can rely on stored
fuel in the muscle,

00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:17.400
so-called glycogen.

00:34:17.400 --> 00:34:19.100
And there are a lot of different ways

00:34:19.100 --> 00:34:20.990
that we can generate ATP.

00:34:20.990 --> 00:34:22.250
So when we ask the question,

00:34:22.250 --> 00:34:25.350
what's limiting for performance?

00:34:25.350 --> 00:34:28.320
What is going to allow us to endure,

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:31.940
to engage in effort and
endure long bouts of effort

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:34.580
or even moderately long bouts of effort?

00:34:34.580 --> 00:34:36.600
We need to ask which of those things,

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:40.660
nerve, muscle, blood, heart
and lungs is limiting?

00:34:40.660 --> 00:34:41.860
Or put differently,

00:34:41.860 --> 00:34:44.080
we ask, what should we be
doing with our neurons?

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:45.420
What should we be doing with our muscles?

00:34:45.420 --> 00:34:47.070
What should we be doing with our blood?

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:48.230
What should we be doing with our heart

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:50.350
and what should we be doing with our lungs

00:34:50.350 --> 00:34:53.970
that's going to allow
us to build endurance

00:34:53.970 --> 00:34:56.020
for mental and physical work

00:34:56.020 --> 00:35:01.020
and to be able to go longer,
further with more intensity?

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:03.990
That's the real question?

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:06.130
How can we do more work?

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:08.030
And the way we do that is with energy,

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:09.250
and the way to get energy to it,

00:35:09.250 --> 00:35:10.550
is to buy those five things.

00:35:10.550 --> 00:35:12.290
And so now we're going to talk about,

00:35:12.290 --> 00:35:16.490
how you can actually build
different types of endurance?

00:35:16.490 --> 00:35:19.810
And what that does at
the level of your blood,

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:21.540
your heart, your muscles and your neurons.

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:25.500
So we're going to skip back and
forth between protocols, tools

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:26.930
and the underlying science.

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:29.570
So rather than heavy stack
the science at the front end

00:35:29.570 --> 00:35:31.270
and then just give you
all the tools at the end,

00:35:31.270 --> 00:35:34.810
we're going to talk about the protocols,

00:35:34.810 --> 00:35:37.250
the four kinds of endurance
and how to achieve them?

00:35:37.250 --> 00:35:39.580
And we are going to talk
about the underlying science

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:41.570
as we move through that.

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:44.220
If you would like a lot
of detailed science,

00:35:44.220 --> 00:35:46.230
I encourage you to check out a review

00:35:46.230 --> 00:35:48.840
that we've linked in the show notes.

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:51.860
And the review is called
adaptations to endurance

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:53.160
and strength training.

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:56.560
This is a review article with
many excellent citations.

00:35:56.560 --> 00:35:59.310
It's from Cold Spring Harbor
Perspectives in Medicine.

00:35:59.310 --> 00:36:00.350
The Cold Spring Harbor Press

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:02.370
is an excellent scientific press.

00:36:02.370 --> 00:36:05.870
Spent the last 21 years doing
summers at Cold Spring Harbor

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:07.260
teaching neuroscience,

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:10.240
but Cold Spring Harbor is
involved in all sorts of themes

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:14.280
and topics related to
neuroscience and medicine.

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:18.630
This review by Hughes L.Ellefson.

00:36:18.630 --> 00:36:19.780
Ellefson that's the name.

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:22.180
Ellefson and Barr, B-A-A-R.

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:24.240
Adaptations through endurance
and strength training

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:26.090
is rich with citations.

00:36:26.090 --> 00:36:28.440
It can be downloaded as a complete PDF.

00:36:28.440 --> 00:36:30.880
There's no paywall and we will link to it.

00:36:30.880 --> 00:36:35.020
And it gets really deep into
all the signaling cascades,

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:37.272
the genetic changes within muscle

00:36:37.272 --> 00:36:39.280
with high intensity interval training,

00:36:39.280 --> 00:36:43.860
short-term, super high intensity
training, weight training.

00:36:43.860 --> 00:36:46.420
So if you're a real nerd for this stuff

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:49.045
and you want to get right
down into how PGC-1alpha,

00:36:49.045 --> 00:36:54.040
P53 and PH20 change the
adaptation features of muscle

00:36:54.040 --> 00:36:55.000
and gene regulation,

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:56.750
that is definitely the review for you.

00:36:56.750 --> 00:36:58.130
If you're like most people,

00:36:58.130 --> 00:37:00.810
and you're not really interested
in that level of detail,

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:02.180
no reason to pick up the review,

00:37:02.180 --> 00:37:03.290
unless you just want to check out

00:37:03.290 --> 00:37:05.550
some of the figures and pictures.

00:37:05.550 --> 00:37:07.830
But I do want to offer that as a resource

00:37:07.830 --> 00:37:11.410
it's been in addition to
discussions with Dr. Andy Galpin,

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:14.100
it's been a primary
resource for the content

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:15.710
of this episode.

00:37:15.710 --> 00:37:18.420
So let's talk about the
four kinds of endurance

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:20.660
and how to achieve those?

00:37:20.660 --> 00:37:23.640
I do believe that everybody
should have some sort

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:27.410
of endurance practice,
regular endurance practice.

00:37:27.410 --> 00:37:30.280
It's clear that it's vital for
the functioning of the body

00:37:30.280 --> 00:37:31.500
and the mind.

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:35.224
And there are clear longevity benefits.

00:37:35.224 --> 00:37:38.130
There are a lot of
reasons why that's true,

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:43.130
but the main one is that if we
have good energy utilization

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:48.340
in our musculature and in our blood,

00:37:48.340 --> 00:37:52.800
in our vascular system and
in our oxygenating system,

00:37:52.800 --> 00:37:55.230
our lungs, the so-called
cardiovascular system,

00:37:55.230 --> 00:37:57.710
respiratory system and musculature.

00:37:57.710 --> 00:38:00.080
The body and brain function much better.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:04.170
There are so many papers now,
so much data to support that.

00:38:04.170 --> 00:38:07.890
So I do believe everyone
should either try to maintain

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:09.600
the muscle that they have,

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:11.270
provided they've already
gone through puberty

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:12.690
and development.

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:16.790
And they should be engaged in
regular endurance exercise.

00:38:16.790 --> 00:38:19.660
Now, for many people, they
think endurance exercise.

00:38:19.660 --> 00:38:21.050
That means what?

00:38:21.050 --> 00:38:24.400
A hour long run or I got
to get on the StairMaster

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:27.330
or I have to treadmill for
hours on end each week.

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:28.690
And it turns out that's not the case.

00:38:28.690 --> 00:38:30.350
There are four kinds of endurance,

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:33.560
and you can train specifically
for any one of those,

00:38:33.560 --> 00:38:35.090
and you can vary your training.

00:38:35.090 --> 00:38:37.400
So let's talk about those
four kinds of endurance.

00:38:37.400 --> 00:38:38.520
These are very interesting

00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:41.790
and they each have very
different protocols

00:38:41.790 --> 00:38:45.480
that you use in order to
build and maximize them.

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:49.440
And now you'll understand
what fuel sources they use

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:51.690
in order to build that
thing we call endurance.

00:38:51.690 --> 00:38:55.490
So first of all, we
have muscular endurance.

00:38:55.490 --> 00:39:00.490
Muscular endurance is the
ability for our muscles

00:39:00.930 --> 00:39:04.250
to perform work over time,

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:08.300
and our failure to continue
to be able to perform

00:39:08.300 --> 00:39:12.410
that work is going to be
due to muscular fatigue,

00:39:12.410 --> 00:39:15.130
not to cardiovascular fatigue.

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:16.850
So not because we're breathing too hard

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:19.160
or we can't get enough
blood to the muscles

00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:21.230
or because we quit mentally,

00:39:21.230 --> 00:39:24.800
but because the muscles
themselves give out.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:25.633
Okay?

00:39:25.633 --> 00:39:28.220
One good example of this would be,

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:32.690
if you had to pick up a stone in the yard

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:36.410
and that stone is not
extremely heavy for you,

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:40.900
and you needed to do that
anywhere from 50 to a 100 times,

00:39:40.900 --> 00:39:42.570
and you were picking it
up and putting it down

00:39:42.570 --> 00:39:43.790
and picking it up and putting it down

00:39:43.790 --> 00:39:45.480
and picking up and putting it down.

00:39:45.480 --> 00:39:48.590
At some point, your muscles will fatigue.

00:39:48.590 --> 00:39:50.770
They will fail to endure.

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:53.870
Muscular endurance is incredibly useful

00:39:53.870 --> 00:39:56.798
for a variety of physical pursuits.

00:39:56.798 --> 00:39:58.850
And we will talk about the mental pursuits

00:39:58.850 --> 00:40:00.570
that it supports as well.

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:02.760
In terms of physical pursuits,

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:06.920
the ability for a given muscle
to perform repeated work

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:09.360
is going to improve your golf swing.

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:11.720
It's going to improve your tennis swing.

00:40:11.720 --> 00:40:13.570
It's going to improve your posture,

00:40:13.570 --> 00:40:14.840
your ability to dance,

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:18.750
your ability to repeatedly
engage in an activity

00:40:18.750 --> 00:40:23.320
that requires effort in a
way that's very different

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:25.840
from the kind of endurance
that you will build simply

00:40:25.840 --> 00:40:28.270
by increasing your cardiovascular fitness,

00:40:28.270 --> 00:40:31.280
your ability to generate
kind of easy repetition.

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:33.980
So let's talk about muscular
endurance and what it is?

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:36.770
Muscular endurance is
going to be something

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:41.770
that you can perform for
anywhere from 12 to 25,

00:40:42.140 --> 00:40:44.760
or even up to 100 repetitions.

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:46.740
And that's actually how if you like

00:40:46.740 --> 00:40:48.760
you would train muscular endurance.

00:40:48.760 --> 00:40:51.920
And I'll give the specific
protocol in a few moments.

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:54.950
So a good example is push-ups, right?

00:40:54.950 --> 00:40:59.660
If you were to get on the
floor and start doing push-ups,

00:40:59.660 --> 00:41:02.840
even if you're somebody who
has to do knees down push-ups,

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:04.180
and you're doing your push-ups,

00:41:04.180 --> 00:41:06.570
eventually you won't be
able to do any more push-ups

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:10.320
and that's not going to be
because you couldn't get enough

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:12.140
oxygen into your system,

00:41:12.140 --> 00:41:14.990
or your heart wasn't pumping enough blood.

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:17.410
It's going to be because the muscles fail.

00:41:17.410 --> 00:41:18.430
That's why.

00:41:18.430 --> 00:41:20.540
So if you want to be
able to do more push-ups

00:41:20.540 --> 00:41:22.180
or even more pull-ups.

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:25.140
Muscular endurance is
really what it's about.

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:28.470
It's actually no coincidence
that a lot of military bootcamp

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:30.760
style training is not done with weights,

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:32.690
it's done with things
like push-ups, pull-ups,

00:41:32.690 --> 00:41:34.030
sit-ups and running.

00:41:34.030 --> 00:41:36.240
Because what they're really
building is muscular endurance.

00:41:36.240 --> 00:41:39.570
The ability to perform
work repeatedly over time

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:42.370
for a given set of muscles and neurons.

00:41:42.370 --> 00:41:44.570
So what's a good protocol
to build muscular endurance.

00:41:44.570 --> 00:41:45.560
Let's just give that to you now

00:41:45.560 --> 00:41:50.560
and explain some of the
underlying science as it follows.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:54.760
So a really good muscular
endurance training protocol

00:41:54.760 --> 00:41:56.760
according to the scientific
literature would be,

00:41:56.760 --> 00:42:01.760
three to five sets of anywhere
from 12 to 100 repetitions.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:04.170
That's a huge range.

00:42:04.170 --> 00:42:09.170
Now, 12 to 25 repetitions is
going to be more reasonable

00:42:10.120 --> 00:42:11.620
for most people.

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:14.240
And the rest periods
are going to be anywhere

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:17.480
from 30 to 180 seconds of rest.

00:42:17.480 --> 00:42:19.920
So anywhere from half a minute
to three minutes of rest.

00:42:19.920 --> 00:42:22.980
So this might be five sets of push-ups,

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:24.420
done getting your maximum push-ups

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:26.530
although for some people
that might be zero

00:42:26.530 --> 00:42:27.870
and you have to do it knees down.

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:29.390
For some people it might be 10 push-ups,

00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:30.540
for some people might be 25,

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:32.200
but you could go all the way up to 100.

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:35.060
Rest anywhere from 30 to 180 seconds,

00:42:35.060 --> 00:42:36.500
and then do your next set

00:42:36.500 --> 00:42:38.420
for a total of three to five sets.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.730
So it doesn't actually
sound like a ton of work.

00:42:40.730 --> 00:42:43.070
The other thing you could do
is something like a plank.

00:42:43.070 --> 00:42:46.530
A plank position is actually a way

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:50.945
to build muscular endurance, not strength.

00:42:50.945 --> 00:42:51.778
Okay?

00:42:51.778 --> 00:42:53.690
I'm sure it could be
used to develop strength,

00:42:53.690 --> 00:42:55.410
but it's really about muscular endurance.

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:57.980
So you would do three
to five sets of planks.

00:42:57.980 --> 00:42:59.660
Those planks would probably even

00:42:59.660 --> 00:43:00.850
because you're not doing repetitions,

00:43:00.850 --> 00:43:02.580
it's an isometric hold as we say,

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:05.000
as kind of static hold or a wall sit

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:06.230
would be another example.

00:43:06.230 --> 00:43:10.100
And you would do that probably
for a minute or two minutes,

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:13.800
take some rest of anywhere
from 30, 60, or 180 seconds,

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:15.020
and then repeat.

00:43:15.020 --> 00:43:17.602
So things like pushing a sled, push-ups,

00:43:17.602 --> 00:43:21.000
isometric planks, even pull-ups,

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:23.490
those will all work.

00:43:23.490 --> 00:43:26.980
And as with others forms of training,

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:28.250
you would want to do this,

00:43:28.250 --> 00:43:31.450
until you approach
failure or actually fail

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:34.110
and where you're unable to
perform another repetition

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:36.740
that would mark the end of a set.

00:43:36.740 --> 00:43:40.060
The one critical feature of
building muscular endurance

00:43:40.060 --> 00:43:44.940
is that it has no major east
centric loading component.

00:43:44.940 --> 00:43:47.990
Now, I haven't talked much about eccentric

00:43:47.990 --> 00:43:49.810
and concentric loading.

00:43:49.810 --> 00:43:53.010
But concentric loading is
when you are shortening

00:43:53.010 --> 00:43:56.030
the muscle typically or lifting a weight.

00:43:56.030 --> 00:43:57.340
And east centric movements

00:43:57.340 --> 00:43:59.890
are when you are lengthening
a muscle typically

00:43:59.890 --> 00:44:01.680
or lowering a weight.

00:44:01.680 --> 00:44:04.810
So if you do a pull up and
you get your chin over the bar

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:08.220
or a chin up, that's the
concentric portion of the effort,

00:44:08.220 --> 00:44:09.330
and then as you lower yourself,

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:11.397
that's the eccentric portion.

00:44:11.397 --> 00:44:15.270
Eccentric portion of resistance
training of any kind,

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:17.290
whether or not it's for
endurance or for strength

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:20.200
is one of the major causes of soreness.

00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:23.710
Some people will be more
susceptible to this,

00:44:23.710 --> 00:44:25.160
excuse me than others,

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:28.960
but it does create more
damage in muscle fibers.

00:44:28.960 --> 00:44:31.840
Muscular endurance and
building muscular endurance

00:44:31.840 --> 00:44:33.610
should not include any movements

00:44:33.610 --> 00:44:36.410
that include major eccentric loads.

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:38.930
So if you're going to do push-ups,

00:44:38.930 --> 00:44:40.340
it doesn't mean that you want to drop,

00:44:40.340 --> 00:44:42.940
you know, smash your chest into the floor.

00:44:42.940 --> 00:44:44.620
And by the way your chest
should touch the ground

00:44:44.620 --> 00:44:46.010
on every push-up.

00:44:46.010 --> 00:44:47.300
That's a real push-up.

00:44:47.300 --> 00:44:48.133
Okay?

00:44:48.133 --> 00:44:49.700
It's not about breaking
90 with the elbows,

00:44:49.700 --> 00:44:52.220
it's about pushing down until
your chest touch the floor

00:44:52.220 --> 00:44:53.053
and straightening out.

00:44:53.053 --> 00:44:54.350
That's a proper push-up.

00:44:54.350 --> 00:44:57.730
And a pull-up is where you pull
up your chin above the bar.

00:44:57.730 --> 00:45:00.730
Neither of those should
include a slow eccentric

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:02.170
or lowering component.

00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:05.050
If you are using those to
train muscular endurance

00:45:05.050 --> 00:45:07.417
the three to five sets of 12 to 25

00:45:07.417 --> 00:45:09.760
and maybe even up to 100 repetitions

00:45:09.760 --> 00:45:14.090
with 3,280 seconds of rest in between.

00:45:14.090 --> 00:45:18.790
That means that jumping also
is going to be a very poor tool

00:45:18.790 --> 00:45:21.520
for building muscular endurance,

00:45:21.520 --> 00:45:25.460
because jumping has a slowing
down component as you land.

00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:28.340
So things like plyometrics or agility work

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:29.730
where you're moving from side to side,

00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:30.790
and you're decelerating,

00:45:30.790 --> 00:45:32.530
you're slowing yourself down a lot,

00:45:32.530 --> 00:45:34.660
not going to be good
for muscular endurance.

00:45:34.660 --> 00:45:37.040
Terrific for cardiovascular training

00:45:37.040 --> 00:45:39.130
and conditioning of other
kinds and skill training

00:45:39.130 --> 00:45:40.680
and agility and all that.

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:43.640
But if you want to build
muscular endurance,

00:45:43.640 --> 00:45:47.960
you want to make your muscles
able to do more work for longer.

00:45:47.960 --> 00:45:49.860
It's going to be this three to five sets

00:45:49.860 --> 00:45:51.979
of 12 to 100 reps,

00:45:51.979 --> 00:45:56.220
30 to 180 seconds of
mainly concentric movement.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:45:57.053
Okay?

00:45:57.053 --> 00:46:02.000
Not a slow lowering phase
or a heavy lowering phase.

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:05.610
So that might be kettlebell
swings and things of that sort.

00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:07.360
Isometrics, as I mentioned,
things like planks

00:46:07.360 --> 00:46:08.940
and wall sets will work.

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:11.400
Now, what's interesting
about this is that,

00:46:11.400 --> 00:46:14.260
it doesn't seem at all
like what people normally

00:46:14.260 --> 00:46:15.600
think of as endurance.

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:18.970
And yet it's been shown in nice, quality

00:46:18.970 --> 00:46:20.070
peer-reviewed studies,

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:21.840
several of which are cited in the review

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:23.230
I mentioned earlier,

00:46:23.230 --> 00:46:27.670
that muscular endurance
can improve our ability

00:46:27.670 --> 00:46:31.630
to engage in long bouts of
what we call long duration,

00:46:31.630 --> 00:46:33.610
low-intensity endurance work.

00:46:33.610 --> 00:46:35.620
So this can support long runs.

00:46:35.620 --> 00:46:37.260
It can support long swims.

00:46:37.260 --> 00:46:40.090
And it can build also...

00:46:40.090 --> 00:46:45.090
It can build postural strength
and endurance simultaneously

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:48.120
and that's mainly accomplished
through isometrical.

00:46:48.120 --> 00:46:51.770
So things like planks
are actually quite good

00:46:51.770 --> 00:46:54.410
for building endurance of
the spinal erector muscles

00:46:54.410 --> 00:46:57.570
that provide posture of
the abdominal muscles

00:46:57.570 --> 00:47:00.250
that are helpful for
posture for being upright

00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:03.470
for the upper neck muscles
and things of that sort.

00:47:03.470 --> 00:47:05.520
These days everyone
seems to have texts neck.

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:07.790
Everyone is basically staring
at their toes all the time

00:47:07.790 --> 00:47:09.610
as a default towards their toes.

00:47:09.610 --> 00:47:11.590
So isometric holds can be very good

00:47:11.590 --> 00:47:13.780
for building muscular endurance.

00:47:13.780 --> 00:47:16.890
You can spot people including yourself,

00:47:16.890 --> 00:47:20.960
perhaps with poor muscular
endurance in the postural muscles

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:22.690
because anytime they stop moving,

00:47:22.690 --> 00:47:24.380
they have to lean against a wall

00:47:24.380 --> 00:47:26.610
or their hip will move to one side,

00:47:26.610 --> 00:47:28.330
or they're always leaned to one side.

00:47:28.330 --> 00:47:29.900
I am guilty of this too.

00:47:29.900 --> 00:47:31.600
Some of you have actually pointed out.

00:47:31.600 --> 00:47:33.410
I like to think out of concern

00:47:33.410 --> 00:47:36.280
that I often am rubbing my lower back.

00:47:36.280 --> 00:47:39.040
And indeed I have some
asymmetries of my postural muscles

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:40.450
some of which are probably genetic

00:47:40.450 --> 00:47:43.322
and some of which are probably
just from excessive work

00:47:43.322 --> 00:47:44.530
or something of that sort

00:47:44.530 --> 00:47:47.010
that have my right shoulder
set lower than my left

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:48.000
and things of that sort.

00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:49.710
If I wanted to improve those,

00:47:49.710 --> 00:47:52.180
I could improve those by
really focusing on symmetry

00:47:52.180 --> 00:47:53.200
and isometric.

00:47:53.200 --> 00:47:56.240
Symmetry meaning holding my
hands at equivalent positions

00:47:56.240 --> 00:47:59.310
in planks and doing isometric holds

00:47:59.310 --> 00:48:03.110
for building muscular endurance
of the postural muscles.

00:48:03.110 --> 00:48:04.750
But this can also be done with,

00:48:04.750 --> 00:48:06.800
as I mentioned, kettlebell
swings for the lower back

00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:08.550
and legs and posterior chain.

00:48:08.550 --> 00:48:09.870
So there are a number
of different exercises

00:48:09.870 --> 00:48:10.703
you could do this with,

00:48:10.703 --> 00:48:14.240
but it should be compound
exercises mainly.

00:48:14.240 --> 00:48:17.400
It's rare for people to do
this kind of muscular endurance

00:48:17.400 --> 00:48:21.660
work specifically for things
like bicep curls or triceps.

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:23.180
And there aren't many activities

00:48:23.180 --> 00:48:25.247
that really rely on
isolation of those muscles

00:48:25.247 --> 00:48:26.690
and repeatedly, right?

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:27.523
It's hard...

00:48:27.523 --> 00:48:28.356
I'm sure there are some out there,

00:48:28.356 --> 00:48:30.220
but it's kind of hard to imagine.

00:48:30.220 --> 00:48:32.600
So you can do this with isometrics,

00:48:32.600 --> 00:48:34.900
you can do this with more standard

00:48:34.900 --> 00:48:36.180
non-isometric type movements,

00:48:36.180 --> 00:48:38.730
but make sure there isn't
a strong eccentric load.

00:48:38.730 --> 00:48:40.540
So now let's talk about
the science briefly

00:48:40.540 --> 00:48:42.490
of why this works?

00:48:42.490 --> 00:48:46.970
Well, that takes us back to
this issue of fuel utilization

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:48.670
and what fails?

00:48:48.670 --> 00:48:52.680
So if we would say, okay,
let's say you do a plank

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:54.480
and you're planking for,

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:56.370
you know, maybe you're
able to plank for a minute

00:48:56.370 --> 00:48:58.360
or two minutes or three minutes.

00:48:58.360 --> 00:49:00.020
At some point you will fail.

00:49:00.020 --> 00:49:02.340
You're not going to fail
because the heart gives out.

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:05.670
You're not going to fail because
you can't get enough oxygen

00:49:05.670 --> 00:49:07.580
because you can breathe
while you're doing that.

00:49:07.580 --> 00:49:10.180
You're going to fail because
of local muscular failure.

00:49:10.180 --> 00:49:11.780
Which means that as you do,

00:49:11.780 --> 00:49:15.260
if you choose to do this
protocol of three to five sets,

00:49:15.260 --> 00:49:18.750
et cetera, et cetera, to
build muscular endurance,

00:49:18.750 --> 00:49:21.760
mainly what you are going to be building

00:49:21.760 --> 00:49:24.280
is you're going to be building the ability

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:27.450
of your mitochondria to use oxygen

00:49:27.450 --> 00:49:29.270
to generate energy locally.

00:49:29.270 --> 00:49:32.590
And that is something called
mitochondrial respiration.

00:49:32.590 --> 00:49:36.270
Respiration because of
the involvement of oxygen.

00:49:36.270 --> 00:49:41.180
And it's also going to
be increasing the extent

00:49:41.180 --> 00:49:44.700
to which the neurons control the muscles

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:48.370
and provide a stimulus for
the muscles to contract.

00:49:48.370 --> 00:49:51.300
But this is independent
of power and strength.

00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:52.133
Okay?

00:49:52.133 --> 00:49:55.370
So even though the low sets
like three to five sets

00:49:55.370 --> 00:49:57.350
and the fact that you're doing repetitions

00:49:57.350 --> 00:49:59.100
and you're going to failure,

00:49:59.100 --> 00:50:02.077
even though it seems to
resemble power and strength

00:50:02.077 --> 00:50:05.020
and hypertrophy type training,
it is distinctly different.

00:50:05.020 --> 00:50:08.260
It's not going to generate
strength, hypertrophy and power.

00:50:08.260 --> 00:50:11.690
It's going to mainly create
this ability to endure

00:50:11.690 --> 00:50:13.760
to continually contract muscles

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:15.980
or repeatedly contract muscles.

00:50:15.980 --> 00:50:16.813
Okay?

00:50:16.813 --> 00:50:19.200
Continually if you're
using isometric holds,

00:50:19.200 --> 00:50:20.220
repeatedly, excuse me,

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:23.520
if you're using repetition type exercise

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:27.720
where there's a contraction
and an extension the muscle

00:50:27.720 --> 00:50:30.840
essentially concentric
and an eccentric portion.

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:34.910
But remember that you want the
eccentric portion to be light

00:50:34.910 --> 00:50:36.350
and relatively fast,

00:50:36.350 --> 00:50:37.660
not so fast that you injure yourself,

00:50:37.660 --> 00:50:40.770
but certainly not
deliberately slowed down.

00:50:40.770 --> 00:50:45.770
It was recommended, I
should say by Andy Galpin

00:50:46.200 --> 00:50:48.860
that you not use Olympic lifts for this,

00:50:48.860 --> 00:50:53.070
because once you get past
eight or 12 or 25 repetitions,

00:50:53.070 --> 00:50:56.240
especially form on those
Olympic lifts is key

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:58.020
for not getting injured.

00:50:58.020 --> 00:51:01.060
And while some people can
perform those sorts of of lifts

00:51:01.060 --> 00:51:03.810
likes snatches and deadlifts
and cleans and jerks

00:51:03.810 --> 00:51:05.400
and overhead presses,

00:51:05.400 --> 00:51:10.140
probably not a great idea if
the goal is to push the body

00:51:10.140 --> 00:51:11.220
to points of fatigue,

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:12.960
because you do open yourself up to injury,

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:14.540
unless you're very skilled at doing that,

00:51:14.540 --> 00:51:15.610
or you have a really good coach

00:51:15.610 --> 00:51:17.930
who can help you guide
through those lifts.

00:51:17.930 --> 00:51:19.810
So that's one form of endurance

00:51:19.810 --> 00:51:20.643
which is muscular endurance.

00:51:20.643 --> 00:51:24.230
And it's mainly going to
rely on neural energy.

00:51:24.230 --> 00:51:26.930
So nerves and muscle.

00:51:26.930 --> 00:51:28.950
And it's not going to rely quite so much

00:51:28.950 --> 00:51:32.640
on what's available in your
blood, your heart or your lungs.

00:51:32.640 --> 00:51:35.680
So now let's talk about the
other extreme of endurance

00:51:35.680 --> 00:51:38.320
which is long duration endurance.

00:51:38.320 --> 00:51:40.731
This is the type that
people typically think about

00:51:40.731 --> 00:51:43.010
when they think about endurance.

00:51:43.010 --> 00:51:46.630
You're talking about a
long run, a long swim,

00:51:46.630 --> 00:51:47.950
a long bike ride.

00:51:47.950 --> 00:51:51.960
Well, how long we'll
anywhere from 12 minutes

00:51:51.960 --> 00:51:55.060
to several hours or
maybe even an entire day,

00:51:55.060 --> 00:51:58.540
maybe eight or nine hours of
hiking or running or biking.

00:51:58.540 --> 00:51:59.770
Some people are actually doing those kinds

00:51:59.770 --> 00:52:02.710
of really long events
marathons, for instance.

00:52:02.710 --> 00:52:05.430
So anything longer than 12 minutes.

00:52:05.430 --> 00:52:10.430
And this type of work
builds on fuel utilization

00:52:10.600 --> 00:52:12.390
in the muscles,

00:52:12.390 --> 00:52:15.730
it builds on the activity
of neurons in the brain

00:52:15.730 --> 00:52:17.250
that are involved in what we call

00:52:17.250 --> 00:52:18.740
central pattern generators.

00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:21.150
We talked about this in a previous episode

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:22.570
or several previous episodes.

00:52:22.570 --> 00:52:24.780
These are groups of
neurons that allow our body

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:27.370
to engage in regular rhythmic effort

00:52:27.370 --> 00:52:29.730
without having to think
about the movement too much.

00:52:29.730 --> 00:52:31.770
So running and stepping or swimming

00:52:31.770 --> 00:52:32.990
if you already know how to swim,

00:52:32.990 --> 00:52:36.040
or peddling on a bike or
walking upstairs and hiking,

00:52:36.040 --> 00:52:38.790
you're not thinking about
right, left, right, left.

00:52:38.790 --> 00:52:42.140
It's all carried out by
central pattern generators.

00:52:42.140 --> 00:52:45.500
This is going to be at
less than a hundred percent

00:52:45.500 --> 00:52:47.850
of your maximum oxygen uptake,

00:52:47.850 --> 00:52:50.550
your VO2 max, I'll talk
about what VO2 max is.

00:52:50.550 --> 00:52:52.740
But I just want to give a
sense of what the protocol is,

00:52:52.740 --> 00:52:54.780
and the underlying science.

00:52:54.780 --> 00:52:56.630
How many sets?

00:52:56.630 --> 00:52:57.510
One.

00:52:57.510 --> 00:53:02.510
Long duration effort is one
set of 12 minutes or longer.

00:53:02.850 --> 00:53:04.180
So you're not counting repetitions.

00:53:04.180 --> 00:53:06.350
I sure hope that if you're
going out on a 30 minute run

00:53:06.350 --> 00:53:07.300
or even a 15 minute run,

00:53:07.300 --> 00:53:10.180
that you're not counting steps,

00:53:10.180 --> 00:53:11.810
that you're not counting pedal strokes,

00:53:11.810 --> 00:53:15.690
that you're not on the rower
counting poles on the rower.

00:53:15.690 --> 00:53:16.680
I suppose you could,

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:18.830
but I think that would be pretty dreadful.

00:53:18.830 --> 00:53:22.500
Seems like a poor utilization
of cognitive brain space.

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:25.970
You're getting into
regular repeated effort

00:53:25.970 --> 00:53:29.720
and your ability to continue that effort

00:53:29.720 --> 00:53:32.920
is going to be dependent
mainly on the efficiency

00:53:32.920 --> 00:53:36.390
of the movement on your
ability to strike a balance

00:53:36.390 --> 00:53:40.060
between the movement itself,

00:53:40.060 --> 00:53:43.790
the generation of the muscular
movements that are required,

00:53:43.790 --> 00:53:47.940
and fuel utilization across
the different sources

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:50.270
of nerve muscle, blood, heart and lungs.

00:53:50.270 --> 00:53:51.240
So let's ask the question.

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:53.160
Why would you fail on a long run?

00:53:53.160 --> 00:53:54.540
Why would you quit?

00:53:54.540 --> 00:53:57.140
Well, as you set out on that long run,

00:53:57.140 --> 00:53:58.900
assuming you have some
glycogen in your liver

00:53:58.900 --> 00:54:01.400
and in your muscles, you're
going to use that energy first,

00:54:01.400 --> 00:54:02.910
even if it's very low intensity.

00:54:02.910 --> 00:54:04.460
I guess we're not talking about sprinting,

00:54:04.460 --> 00:54:06.790
we're talking about heading out the door

00:54:06.790 --> 00:54:09.310
or starting off on a marathon.

00:54:09.310 --> 00:54:11.390
You're starting to assuming
you have some conditioning

00:54:11.390 --> 00:54:12.223
or even if you don't,

00:54:12.223 --> 00:54:13.640
you're going to burn carbohydrate.

00:54:13.640 --> 00:54:15.330
You're going to burn
glucose in the bloodstream,

00:54:15.330 --> 00:54:17.720
you're going to burn carbohydrate
as those muscles contract.

00:54:17.720 --> 00:54:19.100
Those what we call slow twitch muscles.

00:54:19.100 --> 00:54:19.933
They're contracting,

00:54:19.933 --> 00:54:22.150
they start burning up fuel to make ATP

00:54:22.150 --> 00:54:23.963
to continue to contract.

00:54:25.080 --> 00:54:29.430
Your mind is going to
use more or less energy

00:54:29.430 --> 00:54:32.040
depending on how much willpower,

00:54:32.040 --> 00:54:34.640
how much of a fight you have
to get into with yourself

00:54:34.640 --> 00:54:36.160
in order to generate the effort.

00:54:36.160 --> 00:54:37.740
I really want to underscore this.

00:54:37.740 --> 00:54:38.850
If you're somebody that's thinking,

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:39.683
maybe I go for the run,

00:54:39.683 --> 00:54:40.630
maybe I don't go for the run.

00:54:40.630 --> 00:54:41.590
I'll do it at two o'clock.

00:54:41.590 --> 00:54:42.423
Okay, 2:05.

00:54:42.423 --> 00:54:43.970
No, I only want to go on the half hour

00:54:43.970 --> 00:54:45.050
or maybe on the main hour.

00:54:45.050 --> 00:54:46.510
And you're going through all that.

00:54:46.510 --> 00:54:47.343
Guess what?

00:54:47.343 --> 00:54:49.900
You're burning up useful energy

00:54:49.900 --> 00:54:53.650
that you could use either
for the run, for example,

00:54:53.650 --> 00:54:55.060
or for something else.

00:54:55.060 --> 00:54:56.560
When we think about something hard,

00:54:56.560 --> 00:54:58.300
when we ruminate,

00:54:58.300 --> 00:55:02.830
when we perseverate on
an idea or on a decision,

00:55:02.830 --> 00:55:05.810
we are burning neural
energy and neural energy

00:55:05.810 --> 00:55:09.020
is glucose and epinephrin
and all the things

00:55:09.020 --> 00:55:10.470
we talked about before.

00:55:10.470 --> 00:55:14.760
So willpower in part is the ability

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:17.570
to devote resources to things

00:55:17.570 --> 00:55:19.480
and part of that is making decisions

00:55:19.480 --> 00:55:21.440
to just either do it or not do it.

00:55:21.440 --> 00:55:23.410
I'm not of the just do it mindset.

00:55:23.410 --> 00:55:25.770
I think there's a right
time and a place to train.

00:55:25.770 --> 00:55:29.200
But I also think that it is not good.

00:55:29.200 --> 00:55:32.430
In other words, it utilizes
excessive resources

00:55:32.430 --> 00:55:35.450
to churn over decisions excessively,

00:55:35.450 --> 00:55:39.330
and you probably burn
as much cognitive energy

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:42.190
deciding about whether or not
to do a given training or not

00:55:42.190 --> 00:55:45.310
as you do in the actual training.

00:55:45.310 --> 00:55:46.143
Okay?

00:55:46.143 --> 00:55:48.010
So we'll talk more about how
this long duration effort

00:55:48.010 --> 00:55:49.740
can relate to mental performance,

00:55:49.740 --> 00:55:52.200
but the long duration
effort should be one set,

00:55:52.200 --> 00:55:53.630
12 minutes or longer.

00:55:53.630 --> 00:55:56.650
It could go for 30 minutes
or 60 minutes or an hour.

00:55:56.650 --> 00:55:59.100
We'll talk about programming
later in the episode.

00:55:59.100 --> 00:56:00.750
This is going to be less
than a hundred percent

00:56:00.750 --> 00:56:03.510
of your maximum oxygen uptake.

00:56:03.510 --> 00:56:05.390
Your heart rate is not going
to be through the ceiling

00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:06.920
or maxed out.

00:56:06.920 --> 00:56:09.610
But it's all about efficiency of movement.

00:56:09.610 --> 00:56:11.030
That's what you're building.

00:56:11.030 --> 00:56:12.950
When you go out for a
run that's 30 minutes,

00:56:12.950 --> 00:56:17.950
you are building the capacity
to repeat that performance.

00:56:18.110 --> 00:56:20.920
The next time, while being more efficient

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:23.420
actually burning less fuel.

00:56:23.420 --> 00:56:25.770
And that might seem a
little bit counterintuitive,

00:56:25.770 --> 00:56:28.390
but every time you do that run,

00:56:28.390 --> 00:56:30.270
what you're doing is you're building up

00:56:30.270 --> 00:56:32.130
mitochondrial density.

00:56:32.130 --> 00:56:35.550
It's not so much about
mitochondrial oxidation

00:56:35.550 --> 00:56:36.930
and respiration.

00:56:36.930 --> 00:56:39.270
You're building up mitochondrial density.

00:56:39.270 --> 00:56:41.960
You're actually increasing
the amount of ATP

00:56:41.960 --> 00:56:45.180
that you can create for
a given about of effort.

00:56:45.180 --> 00:56:47.500
You're becoming more efficient.

00:56:47.500 --> 00:56:48.333
Okay?

00:56:48.333 --> 00:56:51.990
You're burning less fuel
overall doing the same thing.

00:56:51.990 --> 00:56:54.140
That's really what
these long slow distance

00:56:54.140 --> 00:56:56.610
or long bouts of effort
are really all about.

00:56:56.610 --> 00:56:58.670
Now, why do this long duration effort?

00:56:58.670 --> 00:56:59.860
Why would you want to do it?

00:56:59.860 --> 00:57:00.870
Why is it good for you?

00:57:00.870 --> 00:57:04.530
Well, it does something very important

00:57:04.530 --> 00:57:08.470
which is that it builds the
capillary beds within muscles.

00:57:08.470 --> 00:57:10.330
So let's talk a little
bit about vasculature.

00:57:10.330 --> 00:57:13.100
We haven't done this too much yet,

00:57:13.100 --> 00:57:15.580
but if you have seen the episode

00:57:15.580 --> 00:57:16.940
on supercharging performance.

00:57:16.940 --> 00:57:21.620
We talked about these AVAs
arteriovenous anastomosis

00:57:21.620 --> 00:57:26.620
where blood moves from
arteries directly into veins,

00:57:26.750 --> 00:57:28.700
but that's unusual,

00:57:28.700 --> 00:57:33.700
that only takes place in
the so-called glabrous skin

00:57:33.880 --> 00:57:36.560
of the palms, the face and
the bottoms of the feet.

00:57:36.560 --> 00:57:40.610
Typically, for most all
other areas of the body

00:57:40.610 --> 00:57:42.210
what happens is,

00:57:42.210 --> 00:57:46.450
arteries bring blood to a
given tissue like a muscle,

00:57:46.450 --> 00:57:50.770
and veins return that
blood back to the heart.

00:57:50.770 --> 00:57:52.560
There are exceptions, but in general.

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:56.100
And in between arteries and
veins are these little tiny

00:57:56.100 --> 00:57:58.760
what are called capillary
beds or microcapillaries.

00:57:58.760 --> 00:58:00.650
So these are tiny little avenues,

00:58:00.650 --> 00:58:02.940
like little tiny streams and estuaries

00:58:02.940 --> 00:58:05.490
between the bigger arteries and veins.

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:10.160
Now, those are actually
contained within muscle.

00:58:10.160 --> 00:58:12.640
And what's amazing is
that you can increase

00:58:12.640 --> 00:58:13.760
the number of them.

00:58:13.760 --> 00:58:15.700
You can literally build new capillaries.

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:19.600
You can create new little
streams within your muscles

00:58:19.600 --> 00:58:21.880
and the type of long duration effort

00:58:21.880 --> 00:58:24.240
that I was talking about
before to 12 minutes or more

00:58:24.240 --> 00:58:29.200
of steady effort is very
useful for doing that,

00:58:29.200 --> 00:58:32.760
and is very useful for
increasing the mitochondria,

00:58:32.760 --> 00:58:35.760
the energy producing
elements of the cells,

00:58:35.760 --> 00:58:37.910
the actual muscle cells.

00:58:37.910 --> 00:58:42.910
And the reason is when
blood arrives to muscles

00:58:42.960 --> 00:58:44.560
it has oxygen.

00:58:44.560 --> 00:58:46.610
The muscles are going to
use some of that oxygen.

00:58:46.610 --> 00:58:48.540
And then some of the deoxygenated blood

00:58:48.540 --> 00:58:51.940
is going to be sent back to
the heart and to the lungs.

00:58:51.940 --> 00:58:55.990
Now, the more capillaries that
you build into those muscles,

00:58:55.990 --> 00:58:59.850
the more oxygen available
to those muscles.

00:58:59.850 --> 00:59:02.990
I don't want to get too much
into the physics of fluid flow.

00:59:02.990 --> 00:59:05.800
But basically it's the
difference between taking a hose

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:09.630
and sticking it into some
dirt just directly like

00:59:09.630 --> 00:59:12.550
and turning on the faucet at a given rate,

00:59:12.550 --> 00:59:14.100
the spigot rather,

00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:16.080
or having a bunch of little hoses

00:59:16.080 --> 00:59:17.650
like a sprinkler system that go out

00:59:17.650 --> 00:59:19.290
and irrigate the whole yard.

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:22.650
The irrigation is equivalent
to this capillary bed system.

00:59:22.650 --> 00:59:27.460
And it's very good at using
energy sources within blood.

00:59:27.460 --> 00:59:29.840
So the simple way to think about this is,

00:59:29.840 --> 00:59:31.110
when you go out for a run,

00:59:31.110 --> 00:59:32.810
let's say it's the first
run you've done for a while,

00:59:32.810 --> 00:59:34.680
and you out for 12 or 15 minutes

00:59:34.680 --> 00:59:36.327
and somewhere right around 20 minutes

00:59:36.327 --> 00:59:37.460
you're like, that's it.

00:59:37.460 --> 00:59:38.990
I just can't continue.

00:59:38.990 --> 00:59:42.180
Well, when you come back the
next time to do that run,

00:59:42.180 --> 00:59:46.030
you've built endurance largely

00:59:46.030 --> 00:59:48.280
because you've built these capillary beds.

00:59:48.280 --> 00:59:50.300
You've expanded these little streams

00:59:50.300 --> 00:59:52.970
in which blood can deliver
oxygen to the muscles.

00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:55.730
And so it's going to feel
relatively straightforward

00:59:55.730 --> 00:59:58.810
to either go a little bit
quicker for the same duration,

00:59:58.810 --> 00:59:59.990
the same distance.

00:59:59.990 --> 01:00:04.250
Or to extend that run for
another five to 10 minutes.

01:00:04.250 --> 01:00:06.170
So this long duration work,

01:00:06.170 --> 01:00:08.330
unlike muscular endurance like planks

01:00:08.330 --> 01:00:10.620
and everything that we
were talking about before

01:00:10.620 --> 01:00:13.360
is really about building
the capillary systems

01:00:13.360 --> 01:00:14.750
and the mitochondria,

01:00:14.750 --> 01:00:16.690
the energy utilization systems

01:00:16.690 --> 01:00:19.110
within the muscles themselves.

01:00:19.110 --> 01:00:21.220
And that's very important to understand.

01:00:21.220 --> 01:00:24.150
It's distinctly different
than say building the neurons

01:00:24.150 --> 01:00:25.260
that fire the muscles.

01:00:25.260 --> 01:00:26.520
The neurons are already there,

01:00:26.520 --> 01:00:28.440
they're going to fire
those muscles just fine.

01:00:28.440 --> 01:00:30.390
In fact, if your life depended on it,

01:00:30.390 --> 01:00:32.380
today you could probably run a marathon.

01:00:32.380 --> 01:00:33.430
You'd probably get injured,

01:00:33.430 --> 01:00:35.600
it would be very psychologically
and physically painful.

01:00:35.600 --> 01:00:37.990
I don't recommend you do that
unless you're trained for it.

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:39.940
But if you were to train properly for it,

01:00:39.940 --> 01:00:42.680
if you were to do long
duration bouts of effort

01:00:42.680 --> 01:00:45.010
once or twice a week or three times a week

01:00:45.010 --> 01:00:46.460
pretty soon it would become easy,

01:00:46.460 --> 01:00:49.130
because you're building
these vascular micro beds

01:00:49.130 --> 01:00:51.620
or microvascular beds as they're called.

01:00:51.620 --> 01:00:52.453
Okay?

01:00:52.453 --> 01:00:54.700
So you're able to bring
more energy to the muscles

01:00:54.700 --> 01:00:56.980
and they're able to utilize more energy.

01:00:56.980 --> 01:00:58.630
So that's long duration.

01:00:58.630 --> 01:00:59.700
So we've got muscular endurance

01:00:59.700 --> 01:01:01.430
and we've got long duration endurance.

01:01:01.430 --> 01:01:04.380
And then there are two kinds in between

01:01:04.380 --> 01:01:07.500
that in recent years have
gotten a lot of attention

01:01:07.500 --> 01:01:08.610
and excitement,

01:01:08.610 --> 01:01:10.280
but most people are not distinguishing

01:01:10.280 --> 01:01:13.500
between these two kinds of endurance.

01:01:13.500 --> 01:01:17.060
And that's a shame because
in failing to distinguish

01:01:17.060 --> 01:01:18.540
between the two kinds of what we call

01:01:18.540 --> 01:01:20.270
high-intensity training,

01:01:20.270 --> 01:01:23.300
sometimes called high
intensity interval training.

01:01:23.300 --> 01:01:25.380
Most people, perhaps you,

01:01:25.380 --> 01:01:27.960
are not getting nearly as much physical

01:01:27.960 --> 01:01:31.140
and mental benefit out of
high intensity training

01:01:31.140 --> 01:01:32.130
as you could.

01:01:32.130 --> 01:01:33.730
So I want to talk about the two kinds

01:01:33.730 --> 01:01:36.060
of high-intensity interval training,

01:01:36.060 --> 01:01:38.880
and what each of them does
for your brain and body,

01:01:38.880 --> 01:01:41.570
and what sorts of adaptations they cause?

01:01:41.570 --> 01:01:42.990
Because in doing that,

01:01:42.990 --> 01:01:46.830
you can really start to build
up specific energy systems

01:01:46.830 --> 01:01:49.970
in your brain and body in
ways that are best serve you

01:01:49.970 --> 01:01:52.580
for your cognitive work.

01:01:52.580 --> 01:01:55.900
And for other sorts of things
like strength and speed

01:01:55.900 --> 01:01:59.630
or hypertrophy or for running
marathons for that matter.

01:01:59.630 --> 01:02:02.394
So there are two kinds of
high intensity training

01:02:02.394 --> 01:02:03.630
for endurance.

01:02:03.630 --> 01:02:06.233
Sometimes called high-intensity
interval training.

01:02:07.250 --> 01:02:11.410
One, is anaerobic, so-called
an aerobic endurance,

01:02:11.410 --> 01:02:12.520
so no oxygen.

01:02:12.520 --> 01:02:14.640
And the other is aerobic endurance.

01:02:14.640 --> 01:02:17.890
Both of which qualify as HIIT,

01:02:17.890 --> 01:02:19.150
high intensity interval training.

01:02:19.150 --> 01:02:21.793
So let's talk about
anaerobic endurance first.

01:02:22.730 --> 01:02:26.630
Anaerobic endurance from
a protocol perspective

01:02:26.630 --> 01:02:31.088
is going to be three to 12 sets.

01:02:31.088 --> 01:02:32.320
Okay?

01:02:32.320 --> 01:02:34.765
And these records and I'll talk about

01:02:34.765 --> 01:02:37.160
what the repetitions are,

01:02:37.160 --> 01:02:41.050
are going to be performed
at whatever speed allows you

01:02:41.050 --> 01:02:44.440
to complete the work in good, safe form.

01:02:44.440 --> 01:02:45.273
Okay?

01:02:45.273 --> 01:02:46.550
So it could be fast, it could be slow

01:02:46.550 --> 01:02:48.530
as the work continues,

01:02:48.530 --> 01:02:50.470
your repetitions may slow down,

01:02:50.470 --> 01:02:51.810
or it may speed up.

01:02:51.810 --> 01:02:53.010
Chances are it's going to slow down.

01:02:53.010 --> 01:02:54.790
So what does this work?

01:02:54.790 --> 01:02:56.210
What are the sets look like?

01:02:56.210 --> 01:02:59.060
Remember long slow distance is one set.

01:02:59.060 --> 01:03:01.050
Muscular endurance is three to five sets.

01:03:01.050 --> 01:03:03.140
High-intensity anaerobic endurance

01:03:03.140 --> 01:03:07.290
is going to be somewhere
between three and 12 sets.

01:03:07.290 --> 01:03:11.580
And it's going to have
a ratio of work to rest

01:03:11.580 --> 01:03:15.910
of anywhere from three
to one to one to five.

01:03:15.910 --> 01:03:16.743
Okay?

01:03:16.743 --> 01:03:20.900
So what would a three to
one ratio set look like?

01:03:20.900 --> 01:03:24.190
Well, it's going to be 30
seconds of hard pedaling

01:03:24.190 --> 01:03:28.690
on the bike, for instance,
or running or on the rower.

01:03:28.690 --> 01:03:29.830
These are just examples.

01:03:29.830 --> 01:03:31.260
It could be in the pool, swimming.

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:33.350
It could be any number
of things or air squats,

01:03:33.350 --> 01:03:38.350
or weighted squats if you will
provided you can manage that.

01:03:38.510 --> 01:03:41.450
30 seconds on, 10 seconds off.

01:03:41.450 --> 01:03:43.210
That's a very brief rest.

01:03:43.210 --> 01:03:45.000
So three to one is just a good example

01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:47.453
would be 30 seconds on 10 seconds off.

01:03:48.920 --> 01:03:52.490
The opposite extreme on
that ratio be one to five.

01:03:52.490 --> 01:03:55.360
So 20 seconds on a hundred seconds off.

01:03:55.360 --> 01:03:57.490
So you do the work for 20 seconds

01:03:57.490 --> 01:03:59.280
then you rest a hundred seconds.

01:03:59.280 --> 01:04:00.430
Now, what's the difference?

01:04:00.430 --> 01:04:03.660
What should you do three one ratio?

01:04:03.660 --> 01:04:06.000
So 30 seconds on 10 seconds off,

01:04:06.000 --> 01:04:08.040
or should you do one to five,

01:04:08.040 --> 01:04:10.280
20 seconds on to 100 seconds off?

01:04:10.280 --> 01:04:14.100
Well, that will depend on
whether or not the quality

01:04:14.100 --> 01:04:16.720
of the movement is important to you.

01:04:16.720 --> 01:04:19.280
So let's just take a look
at the three to one ratio.

01:04:19.280 --> 01:04:21.550
So in the three to one ratio,

01:04:21.550 --> 01:04:24.710
if you're going to do 30
seconds of hard peddling

01:04:24.710 --> 01:04:26.220
on a bike followed by 10 seconds.

01:04:26.220 --> 01:04:28.630
So maybe one of these what
they call assault bikes,

01:04:28.630 --> 01:04:31.110
and then you stop for 10
seconds and then repeat,

01:04:31.110 --> 01:04:35.440
chances are you will
be able to do one, two,

01:04:36.320 --> 01:04:39.190
three, four, maybe even
as many as 12 sets,

01:04:39.190 --> 01:04:41.280
if you're really in good condition

01:04:41.280 --> 01:04:42.480
that you'll be able to do all those

01:04:42.480 --> 01:04:45.310
because pedaling on the bike
doesn't require a ton of skill.

01:04:45.310 --> 01:04:47.950
And if you do it incorrectly,

01:04:47.950 --> 01:04:49.830
if your elbow flares out
a little bit or something,

01:04:49.830 --> 01:04:51.550
it's very unlikely that you'll get injured

01:04:51.550 --> 01:04:53.440
unless it's really extreme.

01:04:53.440 --> 01:04:54.273
Okay?

01:04:54.273 --> 01:04:57.230
But the same movement done for
instance, with kettlebells.

01:04:57.230 --> 01:04:59.393
So 30 seconds on 10 seconds off.

01:05:00.460 --> 01:05:02.210
The first set will
probably be in good form.

01:05:02.210 --> 01:05:03.960
The second one will be
in pretty good form,

01:05:03.960 --> 01:05:05.680
but let's say you're getting
to the fifth and sixth set

01:05:05.680 --> 01:05:07.770
and you're going 30
seconds on 10 seconds off

01:05:07.770 --> 01:05:10.610
chances are the quality
of your repetitions

01:05:10.610 --> 01:05:11.930
will degrade significantly.

01:05:11.930 --> 01:05:13.260
And you increase the probability

01:05:13.260 --> 01:05:15.050
that you're going to get injured,

01:05:15.050 --> 01:05:18.070
or that you're going
to damage in some way,

01:05:18.070 --> 01:05:20.100
or that you can't complete the movement

01:05:20.100 --> 01:05:22.350
or that some smaller muscles
like your grip muscles

01:05:22.350 --> 01:05:23.760
might give out.

01:05:23.760 --> 01:05:24.593
Okay?

01:05:24.593 --> 01:05:28.430
So the quality of repetitions
is going to drop considerably

01:05:28.430 --> 01:05:30.745
with the three to one approach.

01:05:30.745 --> 01:05:32.630
If you're just doing it for effort.

01:05:32.630 --> 01:05:34.670
And we'll talk about what
this builds in your system

01:05:34.670 --> 01:05:36.270
in a moment, that's fine.

01:05:36.270 --> 01:05:41.270
But for most people, if
quality of form is important,

01:05:41.340 --> 01:05:43.430
so maybe this is using weights,

01:05:43.430 --> 01:05:44.700
maybe you're doing squats.

01:05:44.700 --> 01:05:47.570
You're going to do 20 seconds
on and 100 seconds of rest.

01:05:47.570 --> 01:05:49.320
Maybe it's even a barbell loaded squat,

01:05:49.320 --> 01:05:50.850
maybe you're doing kettlebells,

01:05:50.850 --> 01:05:53.960
maybe you've got some
other resistance there

01:05:53.960 --> 01:05:55.720
that's allowing you to do this.

01:05:55.720 --> 01:05:57.630
What you'll find is that the longer rest,

01:05:57.630 --> 01:06:00.320
even though it's 20
seconds of intense effort,

01:06:00.320 --> 01:06:02.470
followed by a longer rest
of about a hundred seconds

01:06:02.470 --> 01:06:05.580
will allow you to perform
more quality repetitions

01:06:05.580 --> 01:06:07.810
safely over time.

01:06:07.810 --> 01:06:12.727
So what does building
anaerobic endurance look like?

01:06:12.727 --> 01:06:15.400
And then I'll tell you what
it's actually good for,

01:06:15.400 --> 01:06:17.710
in the true practical sense.

01:06:17.710 --> 01:06:22.440
What anaerobic endurance
exercise generally looks like

01:06:22.440 --> 01:06:25.150
is that if you decide to
do this for the first week,

01:06:25.150 --> 01:06:27.140
you might do this two
or three times a week,

01:06:27.140 --> 01:06:28.630
maybe even just once a week,

01:06:28.630 --> 01:06:29.910
depending on the other
things you're doing,

01:06:29.910 --> 01:06:31.850
we'll talk about programming at the end.

01:06:31.850 --> 01:06:34.110
And you would generate just three sets.

01:06:34.110 --> 01:06:37.520
So it might be three sets of
20 seconds of hard effort,

01:06:37.520 --> 01:06:39.410
followed by 100 seconds rest.

01:06:39.410 --> 01:06:41.000
Then you repeat 20 seconds of hard effort,

01:06:41.000 --> 01:06:43.967
100 seconds rest, 20 seconds
of effort, 100 seconds rest,

01:06:43.967 --> 01:06:46.290
and you might do that twice a week.

01:06:46.290 --> 01:06:50.800
And then each week you're
adding one or two sets, okay?

01:06:50.800 --> 01:06:53.320
In doing that, you will
build up what we call

01:06:53.320 --> 01:06:54.820
anaerobic endurance.

01:06:54.820 --> 01:06:56.830
What is anaerobic endurance?

01:06:56.830 --> 01:06:59.370
Well, let's ask why we fail?

01:06:59.370 --> 01:07:03.930
Anaerobic endurance is going
to be taking your system

01:07:03.930 --> 01:07:08.470
into greater than 100% of your VO2 max.

01:07:08.470 --> 01:07:11.380
It's going to be taking
your heart rate up very high

01:07:12.370 --> 01:07:17.370
and it's going to maximize your
oxygen utilization systems.

01:07:17.730 --> 01:07:20.300
That is going to have effects

01:07:20.300 --> 01:07:23.880
that are going to lead
to fatigue at some point

01:07:23.880 --> 01:07:24.713
in the workout.

01:07:24.713 --> 01:07:27.240
And that fatigue will
trigger an adaptation.

01:07:27.240 --> 01:07:30.350
So let's ask what
adaptation it's triggering.

01:07:30.350 --> 01:07:35.120
Well, it's triggering both
mitochondrial respiration,

01:07:35.120 --> 01:07:38.240
the ability of your mitochondria
to generate more energy

01:07:38.240 --> 01:07:40.840
by using more oxygen because
you're bringing so...

01:07:40.840 --> 01:07:41.900
You're maxing out,

01:07:41.900 --> 01:07:44.230
literally you're getting
above your VO2 max.

01:07:44.230 --> 01:07:46.690
You're hitting that
threshold of how much oxygen

01:07:46.690 --> 01:07:48.050
you can use in your system?

01:07:48.050 --> 01:07:50.720
One of the adaptations will
be that your mitochondria

01:07:50.720 --> 01:07:55.370
will shift such that
they can use more oxygen.

01:07:55.370 --> 01:08:00.080
And you're going to also
increase the capillary beds,

01:08:00.080 --> 01:08:02.930
but not as much as you're going to be able

01:08:02.930 --> 01:08:07.630
to increase the amount of
neuron engagement of muscle.

01:08:07.630 --> 01:08:10.020
So normally when we start to hit fatigue,

01:08:10.020 --> 01:08:11.210
when we're exhausted,

01:08:11.210 --> 01:08:12.790
when we're breathing really hard

01:08:12.790 --> 01:08:14.410
because the systems of the body are linked

01:08:14.410 --> 01:08:16.500
and we there's a
component to this as well,

01:08:16.500 --> 01:08:18.840
a kind of motivational component.

01:08:18.840 --> 01:08:21.740
After that third or
fourth or sixth set of,

01:08:21.740 --> 01:08:23.620
you know, 20 seconds on
a hundred seconds off,

01:08:23.620 --> 01:08:25.560
or if you're at the other
extreme 30 seconds on,

01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:26.830
and 10 seconds off.

01:08:26.830 --> 01:08:29.860
There's going to be a
component of you want to stop.

01:08:29.860 --> 01:08:32.960
And by pushing through and
repeating another set safely,

01:08:32.960 --> 01:08:34.070
of course.

01:08:34.070 --> 01:08:37.460
What you're doing is
you're training the neurons

01:08:37.460 --> 01:08:41.150
to be able to access
more energy, literally,

01:08:41.150 --> 01:08:43.430
convert that into ATP and for the muscles

01:08:43.430 --> 01:08:45.960
therefore to access more energy and ATP.

01:08:45.960 --> 01:08:49.520
And the adaptation is in
the mitochondria's ability

01:08:49.520 --> 01:08:50.950
to use oxygen,

01:08:50.950 --> 01:08:53.520
and this has tremendous carry over effects

01:08:53.520 --> 01:08:55.750
for other types of exercise.

01:08:55.750 --> 01:08:58.020
So while I know and appreciate

01:08:58.020 --> 01:09:00.130
that people are using high
intensity interval training

01:09:00.130 --> 01:09:04.700
of this kind or similar in
order to just like burn fat,

01:09:04.700 --> 01:09:07.600
you know, do their
workouts, quote unquote.

01:09:07.600 --> 01:09:11.840
It's very useful for
building a capacity to engage

01:09:11.840 --> 01:09:16.220
in short bouts of effort
repeatedly to really lock in.

01:09:16.220 --> 01:09:17.480
I don't want to use the word focus

01:09:17.480 --> 01:09:19.010
because it's not strictly mental focus,

01:09:19.010 --> 01:09:24.010
but to be able to generate
short bouts of very intense work

01:09:24.150 --> 01:09:26.830
this can be beneficial
in competitive sports

01:09:26.830 --> 01:09:28.880
or team sports where there's
a sprinting component,

01:09:28.880 --> 01:09:30.480
where the field opens up
and you need to dribble

01:09:30.480 --> 01:09:33.030
the ball down the field, for
instance and shoot on goal.

01:09:33.030 --> 01:09:34.450
Or where you're playing tennis,

01:09:34.450 --> 01:09:35.680
and it's a long rally.

01:09:35.680 --> 01:09:38.600
And then all of a sudden,
somebody really starts,

01:09:38.600 --> 01:09:40.220
you know, putting you back on your heels

01:09:40.220 --> 01:09:42.910
and you have to really make
the maximum amount of effort

01:09:42.910 --> 01:09:45.440
to run to the net and to
get the ball across in that.

01:09:45.440 --> 01:09:47.130
Things of that sort.

01:09:47.130 --> 01:09:48.240
Okay?

01:09:48.240 --> 01:09:50.330
There are a variety of places
where there's carry over

01:09:50.330 --> 01:09:51.710
from this type of training,

01:09:51.710 --> 01:09:53.770
but it does support endurance.

01:09:53.770 --> 01:09:55.170
It's about muscle endurance.

01:09:55.170 --> 01:09:58.850
It's about these muscles ability
to generate a lot of force

01:09:58.850 --> 01:10:01.010
in the short-term, but repeatedly.

01:10:01.010 --> 01:10:01.843
Okay?

01:10:01.843 --> 01:10:03.060
So that's the way to conceptualize this.

01:10:03.060 --> 01:10:05.560
And it is different than maximum power.

01:10:05.560 --> 01:10:07.680
Even though it feels like maximum effort,

01:10:07.680 --> 01:10:10.900
it is not the same as building
power and speed into muscles.

01:10:10.900 --> 01:10:13.270
Those are distinctly different protocols.

01:10:13.270 --> 01:10:15.450
So the key elements again

01:10:15.450 --> 01:10:18.320
are that you're bringing your breathing

01:10:18.320 --> 01:10:22.300
and your oxygen utilization
way up above your max.

01:10:22.300 --> 01:10:24.500
It's not quite hitting failure,

01:10:24.500 --> 01:10:26.720
but you're really pushing the system

01:10:26.720 --> 01:10:29.800
to the point where you are
not ready to do another set

01:10:29.800 --> 01:10:31.320
and yet you begin another set.

01:10:31.320 --> 01:10:33.840
You're not necessarily
psychologically ready.

01:10:33.840 --> 01:10:35.660
I'll talk more about
some of the adaptations

01:10:35.660 --> 01:10:39.030
that this causes in terms of
stroke volume in a few minutes

01:10:39.030 --> 01:10:42.190
when we talk about how it
is that work of this sort

01:10:42.190 --> 01:10:45.770
can increase our heart's ability
to deliver blood and oxygen

01:10:45.770 --> 01:10:47.210
to our lungs and other tissues.

01:10:47.210 --> 01:10:50.090
I'm going to get very
specific about how to breathe

01:10:50.090 --> 01:10:51.910
during these different types of protocols

01:10:51.910 --> 01:10:53.410
and what's happening at
the level of the heart?

01:10:53.410 --> 01:10:55.870
But I want to make sure I
touch on the fourth protocol

01:10:55.870 --> 01:10:59.140
which is high intensity
aerobic conditioning.

01:10:59.140 --> 01:11:02.200
So HIIT has these two forms,
anaerobic and aerobic.

01:11:02.200 --> 01:11:04.920
And you just heard about anaerobic.

01:11:04.920 --> 01:11:07.410
High-intensity aerobic conditioning

01:11:07.410 --> 01:11:10.133
also involves about three to 12 sets.

01:11:11.310 --> 01:11:13.620
Starting off of course, with fewer sets

01:11:13.620 --> 01:11:14.800
as you're getting into this training

01:11:14.800 --> 01:11:17.090
and then extending into more sets

01:11:17.090 --> 01:11:19.470
as one parameter you could expand.

01:11:19.470 --> 01:11:22.430
Has again the same ratio of three to one.

01:11:22.430 --> 01:11:23.990
So 30 seconds on 10 seconds off,

01:11:23.990 --> 01:11:26.770
or one to five, 20 seconds
on 100 seconds off,

01:11:26.770 --> 01:11:30.110
or a very powerful tool for
building up aerobic conditioning

01:11:30.110 --> 01:11:32.850
is a one-to-one ratio.

01:11:32.850 --> 01:11:37.850
A one-to-one ratio is powerful
for building on average.

01:11:39.330 --> 01:11:42.050
Most of the energy systems involving,

01:11:42.050 --> 01:11:46.223
remember we had these nerve
muscle blood, heart and lungs.

01:11:47.372 --> 01:11:50.820
A one-to-one ratio
might be you run a mile.

01:11:50.820 --> 01:11:52.140
And however long that takes,

01:11:52.140 --> 01:11:54.280
let's say it takes you six
minutes or seven minutes,

01:11:54.280 --> 01:11:56.693
then you rest for an
equivalent amount of time.

01:11:57.560 --> 01:11:58.793
Then you repeat.

01:11:59.760 --> 01:12:01.430
And then you rest for an
equivalent amount of time.

01:12:01.430 --> 01:12:04.380
So you might run first mile
is let's say seven minutes.

01:12:04.380 --> 01:12:05.470
Then you rest for seven minutes.

01:12:05.470 --> 01:12:06.510
Then you run a mile again,

01:12:06.510 --> 01:12:08.167
and it might take eight minutes

01:12:08.167 --> 01:12:09.390
and you rest for eight minutes.

01:12:09.390 --> 01:12:12.623
And you continue that for a
total of four miles of work.

01:12:13.530 --> 01:12:16.470
Four miles of running work, I should say.

01:12:16.470 --> 01:12:17.920
Or seven miles of work.

01:12:17.920 --> 01:12:19.060
You can build this up.

01:12:19.060 --> 01:12:22.570
Many people find that
using this type of training

01:12:22.570 --> 01:12:26.140
allows them to do things
like go run half marathons

01:12:26.140 --> 01:12:29.600
and marathons even though
prior to the race date,

01:12:29.600 --> 01:12:32.750
they've never actually run
a half marathon or marathon.

01:12:32.750 --> 01:12:34.790
Now, that might seem incredible.

01:12:34.790 --> 01:12:37.680
It's like how could it be
that running a mile on,

01:12:37.680 --> 01:12:39.660
and then resting for running a mile

01:12:39.660 --> 01:12:41.540
and then resting for an
equivalent amount of time,

01:12:41.540 --> 01:12:43.660
running a mile, resting
for equivalent amount time

01:12:43.660 --> 01:12:46.930
for seven miles allows
you to run continuously

01:12:46.930 --> 01:12:50.460
for 13 miles or for 26 miles.

01:12:50.460 --> 01:12:52.230
Well, I'm not discouraging people

01:12:52.230 --> 01:12:54.700
from ever doing the
long duration endurance.

01:12:54.700 --> 01:12:56.450
I think that is very important.

01:12:56.450 --> 01:13:00.630
But it's because it builds
up so many of these energy

01:13:00.630 --> 01:13:01.810
utilization systems.

01:13:01.810 --> 01:13:04.650
It really teaches you
to engage, excuse me,

01:13:04.650 --> 01:13:06.810
the nerve to muscle firing.

01:13:06.810 --> 01:13:11.810
It improves ATP and
mitochondrial function in muscle.

01:13:12.310 --> 01:13:15.410
It allows the blood to deliver more oxygen

01:13:15.410 --> 01:13:17.940
to the muscle and to your brain.

01:13:17.940 --> 01:13:19.870
And I'll explain how that is.

01:13:19.870 --> 01:13:24.450
And it allows your heart to
deliver more oxygen overall.

01:13:24.450 --> 01:13:26.660
And it builds a tremendous lung capacity.

01:13:26.660 --> 01:13:28.560
And we will talk about
exactly how to breathe

01:13:28.560 --> 01:13:30.440
and how to build lung capacity.

01:13:30.440 --> 01:13:32.730
Both for sake of warming
up and for performance.

01:13:32.730 --> 01:13:33.770
So what would this look like?

01:13:33.770 --> 01:13:35.960
And when should you do this?

01:13:35.960 --> 01:13:39.560
Well, it's really a
question for these workouts

01:13:39.560 --> 01:13:43.440
of asking how much work can
one do in eight to 12 minutes,

01:13:43.440 --> 01:13:44.273
right?

01:13:44.273 --> 01:13:45.600
And then rest, and then repeat.

01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:47.290
How much work can you do
for eight to 12 minutes

01:13:47.290 --> 01:13:48.240
then rest and then repeat?

01:13:48.240 --> 01:13:50.160
And how many times should you do this?

01:13:50.160 --> 01:13:52.910
Well, this is the sort of
thing it's pretty intense.

01:13:52.910 --> 01:13:56.100
And so you would probably
only want to do this two,

01:13:56.100 --> 01:13:57.270
maybe three times a week

01:13:57.270 --> 01:13:58.810
if you're not doing many other things.

01:13:58.810 --> 01:14:00.390
I will talk about how this program

01:14:00.390 --> 01:14:02.670
can be moved in with
other forms of training.

01:14:02.670 --> 01:14:04.510
But I'll just give you a little hint now.

01:14:04.510 --> 01:14:09.310
It's very clear and it's
described in the review article

01:14:09.310 --> 01:14:12.330
are referred to and we will
link another article as well.

01:14:12.330 --> 01:14:14.827
That concurrent training,
doing strength training

01:14:14.827 --> 01:14:17.320
and the endurance training
of any of the four kinds

01:14:17.320 --> 01:14:19.770
that I'm describing today can be done.

01:14:19.770 --> 01:14:21.730
You can program those in the same week.

01:14:21.730 --> 01:14:24.980
But you want to get four and ideally six

01:14:24.980 --> 01:14:28.290
or even better 24 hours
between these workouts.

01:14:28.290 --> 01:14:30.750
Because it is very hard, for instance,

01:14:30.750 --> 01:14:33.420
to do a one-to-one mile repeats

01:14:33.420 --> 01:14:35.107
like run a mile rest for equivalent time,

01:14:35.107 --> 01:14:36.870
run a mile rest for equivalent time

01:14:36.870 --> 01:14:38.600
to do that two or three times a week,

01:14:38.600 --> 01:14:40.440
and also do weight training before

01:14:40.440 --> 01:14:42.380
or do a long run afterwards.

01:14:42.380 --> 01:14:45.110
That would quickly lead to
break down for most people,

01:14:45.110 --> 01:14:48.990
unless you have very, very good
energy utilization systems.

01:14:48.990 --> 01:14:52.100
You're really kind of
advanced or elite athlete,

01:14:52.100 --> 01:14:55.510
and or dare I say you're using tools

01:14:55.510 --> 01:14:57.710
to enhance your performance
at the level of blood

01:14:57.710 --> 01:14:58.543
or hormones.

01:14:58.543 --> 01:15:00.150
And I'm actually going to
talk about those at the end

01:15:00.150 --> 01:15:01.600
and why they work?

01:15:01.600 --> 01:15:03.320
So we have four kinds of endurance.

01:15:03.320 --> 01:15:04.380
Muscular endurance.

01:15:04.380 --> 01:15:05.970
We have long-duration endurance.

01:15:05.970 --> 01:15:08.140
We have high intensity
interval training of two kinds,

01:15:08.140 --> 01:15:09.830
anaerobic and aerobic.

01:15:09.830 --> 01:15:13.350
And this last type, the
aerobic one works best.

01:15:13.350 --> 01:15:15.550
It seems if you kind of
do this one-to-one ratio.

01:15:15.550 --> 01:15:18.350
So how would you use these and
what are they actually doing?

01:15:18.350 --> 01:15:21.880
Let's talk about the heart
and the lungs and oxygen,

01:15:21.880 --> 01:15:24.540
because that's something
that we can all benefit

01:15:24.540 --> 01:15:25.930
from understanding.

01:15:25.930 --> 01:15:28.670
And it will become very
clear in that discussion

01:15:28.670 --> 01:15:31.300
why this type of training is very useful

01:15:31.300 --> 01:15:34.830
even for non-athletes in
order to improve oxygenation

01:15:34.830 --> 01:15:38.530
and energy utilization of
the brain and the heart.

01:15:38.530 --> 01:15:40.110
The brain and the heart are probably

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:41.470
the two most important systems

01:15:41.470 --> 01:15:43.340
that you need to take
care of in your life.

01:15:43.340 --> 01:15:46.170
Yes, your musculature
needs to be maintained.

01:15:46.170 --> 01:15:47.970
If you want to build it, that's up to you.

01:15:47.970 --> 01:15:50.070
But you should try and
maintain your musculature,

01:15:50.070 --> 01:15:53.070
but maintaining or
enhancing a brain function

01:15:53.070 --> 01:15:54.790
and cardiovascular function.

01:15:54.790 --> 01:15:57.785
It's absolutely clear are
key for health and longevity

01:15:57.785 --> 01:15:59.300
in the short and long-term.

01:15:59.300 --> 01:16:01.210
And the sorts of training
I talked about today

01:16:01.210 --> 01:16:03.210
has been shown again and again and again

01:16:03.210 --> 01:16:07.680
to be very useful for enhancing
the strength of the mind.

01:16:07.680 --> 01:16:09.270
Yes, I'll talk about that.

01:16:09.270 --> 01:16:12.580
As well as the health of
the brain and the body.

01:16:12.580 --> 01:16:14.520
So let's talk about the
sorts of adaptations

01:16:14.520 --> 01:16:16.130
that are happening in your brain and body

01:16:16.130 --> 01:16:19.980
that are so beneficial in these
different forms of training.

01:16:19.980 --> 01:16:23.150
If you are breathing hard and
your heart is beating hard.

01:16:23.150 --> 01:16:26.670
So this would be certainly
in the high intensity

01:16:26.670 --> 01:16:28.690
anaerobic and aerobic conditioning.

01:16:28.690 --> 01:16:30.860
'Cause you're getting up near your VO2 max

01:16:30.860 --> 01:16:32.980
in high intensity aerobic conditioning,

01:16:32.980 --> 01:16:36.130
and you're exceeding your
VO2 max in high intensity

01:16:36.130 --> 01:16:38.720
anaerobic conditioning.

01:16:38.720 --> 01:16:40.560
What's going to happen is as of course,

01:16:40.560 --> 01:16:42.360
your heart beats faster,

01:16:42.360 --> 01:16:46.183
your blood is going to be
circulating faster in principle.

01:16:47.030 --> 01:16:50.280
Oxygen utilization in
muscles is going to go up

01:16:51.310 --> 01:16:54.140
and over time, not long,

01:16:54.140 --> 01:16:55.540
very quickly what will happen

01:16:55.540 --> 01:16:57.860
when those capillary beds start to expand?

01:16:57.860 --> 01:16:58.913
We talked about that.

01:17:00.370 --> 01:17:05.340
But in addition, because
of the amount of blood

01:17:05.340 --> 01:17:07.250
that's being returned to the heart,

01:17:07.250 --> 01:17:09.640
when you engage in these
really intense bouts

01:17:09.640 --> 01:17:12.360
of effort repeatedly,

01:17:12.360 --> 01:17:14.890
the amount of blood being
returned to the heart

01:17:14.890 --> 01:17:18.180
actually causes an east centric loading

01:17:18.180 --> 01:17:21.530
of one of the muscular walls of the heart.

01:17:21.530 --> 01:17:22.680
So your heart is muscle.

01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:23.910
It's cardiac muscle.

01:17:23.910 --> 01:17:25.440
We have skeletal muscle
attached to our bones

01:17:25.440 --> 01:17:28.390
and we have cardiac
muscle, which is our heart.

01:17:28.390 --> 01:17:31.140
When more blood is being
returned to the heart

01:17:31.140 --> 01:17:34.630
because of the additional
work that your muscles

01:17:34.630 --> 01:17:35.873
and nerves are doing,

01:17:37.160 --> 01:17:39.490
it actually has the effect of creating

01:17:39.490 --> 01:17:44.490
an east centric loading, a
kind of pushing of the wall,

01:17:44.870 --> 01:17:46.010
the left wall.

01:17:46.010 --> 01:17:48.040
I realize I'm not using
the strict anatomy here,

01:17:48.040 --> 01:17:50.010
but I don't want to get
into all the features

01:17:50.010 --> 01:17:52.580
of the structural features of the heart.

01:17:52.580 --> 01:17:57.580
But the left ventricle
essentially getting slammed back

01:17:58.030 --> 01:18:00.580
and then having to push back

01:18:00.580 --> 01:18:03.240
in a kind of east centric
loading of the cardiac muscle

01:18:03.240 --> 01:18:05.193
and the muscle thickens,

01:18:06.410 --> 01:18:09.060
but not because the
heart thickens overall,

01:18:09.060 --> 01:18:12.010
it's actually a strengthening
of the cardiac muscle

01:18:12.010 --> 01:18:15.130
in a way that increases
what we call stroke volume.

01:18:15.130 --> 01:18:18.040
Meaning as more blood is
returned to the heart.

01:18:18.040 --> 01:18:20.050
There's an adaptation
where the heart muscle

01:18:20.050 --> 01:18:24.570
actually gets stronger and
therefore can pump more blood

01:18:24.570 --> 01:18:26.400
per stroke per beat.

01:18:26.400 --> 01:18:29.890
And as it does that, it delivers

01:18:29.890 --> 01:18:34.130
because blood contains glucose
and oxygen and other things.

01:18:34.130 --> 01:18:36.400
It delivers more fuel to your muscles

01:18:36.400 --> 01:18:40.160
which allows you to do yet
more work per unit time.

01:18:40.160 --> 01:18:40.993
Okay?

01:18:40.993 --> 01:18:43.040
So when we hear that,
oh, you know, so-and-so

01:18:43.040 --> 01:18:46.810
has a or maybe you have
a nice low heart rate

01:18:46.810 --> 01:18:49.010
that maybe you're one of
these really extreme folks

01:18:49.010 --> 01:18:50.640
like 30 or 40 beats per minute,

01:18:50.640 --> 01:18:53.140
although most people are
sitting at 50, 60, 70, 80,

01:18:53.140 --> 01:18:54.720
that's your resting heart rate.

01:18:54.720 --> 01:18:56.210
If you exercise regularly

01:18:56.210 --> 01:18:59.040
and you do long duration aerobic work,

01:18:59.040 --> 01:19:01.820
your heart rate will start to go down,

01:19:01.820 --> 01:19:02.890
your resting heart rate.

01:19:02.890 --> 01:19:05.410
It will increase the stroke
volume of your heart.

01:19:05.410 --> 01:19:07.600
If you do this high
intensity type training

01:19:07.600 --> 01:19:09.370
where your heart is beating very hard.

01:19:09.370 --> 01:19:12.800
So maybe the one-to-one
ratio mile run repeats

01:19:12.800 --> 01:19:14.080
that I described a minute ago.

01:19:14.080 --> 01:19:17.270
Let's say you do that twice
a week for three or four.

01:19:17.270 --> 01:19:20.130
And I said, it could go all
the way up to 12 rep sets,

01:19:20.130 --> 01:19:20.963
which is a lot.

01:19:20.963 --> 01:19:22.560
I don't recommend people start there.

01:19:22.560 --> 01:19:25.220
Pretty soon, the stroke
volume of your heart

01:19:25.220 --> 01:19:26.340
will really increase.

01:19:26.340 --> 01:19:28.900
And as a consequence,
you can deliver more fuel

01:19:28.900 --> 01:19:31.490
to your muscles and to your brain,

01:19:31.490 --> 01:19:35.490
and you will notice that
you can do more work

01:19:35.490 --> 01:19:37.640
meaning you can do the same
work you were doing a few days

01:19:37.640 --> 01:19:40.230
or weeks ago with relative ease.

01:19:40.230 --> 01:19:42.930
Your cognitive functioning will improve.

01:19:42.930 --> 01:19:44.820
This has been shown again and again,

01:19:44.820 --> 01:19:47.000
because there's an
increase in vasculature.

01:19:47.000 --> 01:19:48.870
Literally, capillary
beds within the brain,

01:19:48.870 --> 01:19:51.540
the hippocampus areas that support memory,

01:19:51.540 --> 01:19:54.700
but also areas of the brain
that support respiration,

01:19:54.700 --> 01:19:55.890
that support focus,

01:19:55.890 --> 01:19:57.360
that support effort.

01:19:57.360 --> 01:19:58.660
This isn't often discussed,

01:19:58.660 --> 01:20:00.830
but the ability to deliver more blood

01:20:00.830 --> 01:20:02.310
and therefore more glucose,

01:20:02.310 --> 01:20:05.700
remember neurons run on
glucose and oxygen to the brain

01:20:05.700 --> 01:20:08.810
is a big feature of why
exercise of the kind

01:20:08.810 --> 01:20:11.840
of describing helps with brain function.

01:20:11.840 --> 01:20:15.670
Now, weight training does
have some positive effects

01:20:15.670 --> 01:20:16.990
on brain function also.

01:20:16.990 --> 01:20:18.980
When I say weight training, I'm really...

01:20:18.980 --> 01:20:20.200
I should be more specific.

01:20:20.200 --> 01:20:23.460
I really am referring to strength
and hypertrophy training.

01:20:23.460 --> 01:20:25.640
Strength and hypertrophy training

01:20:25.640 --> 01:20:28.420
especially if it's of the sort
where you get into the burn

01:20:28.420 --> 01:20:29.700
as we talked about last episode

01:20:29.700 --> 01:20:32.130
and you start generating
lactate as a hormonal signal

01:20:32.130 --> 01:20:34.010
that can benefit your brain, et cetera.

01:20:34.010 --> 01:20:36.690
It can have positive effects on the brain.

01:20:36.690 --> 01:20:38.930
And frankly, there haven't
been as many studies

01:20:38.930 --> 01:20:40.577
of resistance training strength

01:20:40.577 --> 01:20:43.060
and hypertrophy training
on brain function,

01:20:43.060 --> 01:20:45.130
mainly because most of those experiments

01:20:45.130 --> 01:20:48.670
are done in mice or
primates, non-human primates,

01:20:48.670 --> 01:20:49.503
I should say.

01:20:49.503 --> 01:20:53.060
And it's hard to get mice
to do resistance training.

01:20:53.060 --> 01:20:53.893
Okay?

01:20:53.893 --> 01:20:55.250
It's hard to get humans
to do resistance training.

01:20:55.250 --> 01:20:57.140
It's definitely hard to get
mice to do resistance training.

01:20:57.140 --> 01:20:58.160
There are ways to do it,

01:20:58.160 --> 01:21:00.630
but it's hard to get
them to do say, you know,

01:21:00.630 --> 01:21:03.580
three sets of eight on the
deadlift and then do some curls

01:21:03.580 --> 01:21:05.500
and then do some chin-ups
and this kind of thing.

01:21:05.500 --> 01:21:06.453
Okay?

01:21:06.453 --> 01:21:08.550
It's pretty easy to get a
mouse to run on a treadmill

01:21:08.550 --> 01:21:10.980
and you can set the
tension on that treadmill

01:21:10.980 --> 01:21:13.780
to make it so that it's
easier or harder for the mouse

01:21:13.780 --> 01:21:15.540
to turn that wheel.

01:21:15.540 --> 01:21:17.470
So that's one of the reasons.

01:21:17.470 --> 01:21:19.570
However, it's very clear

01:21:19.570 --> 01:21:21.580
and you should now understand intuitively

01:21:21.580 --> 01:21:24.550
why the kind of standard
strength and hypertrophy

01:21:24.550 --> 01:21:28.020
type workouts are not going to activate

01:21:28.020 --> 01:21:31.700
the blood oxygenation and
the stroke volume increases

01:21:31.700 --> 01:21:33.710
for the heart that the sorts of training

01:21:33.710 --> 01:21:35.410
I'm talking about today will.

01:21:35.410 --> 01:21:38.730
It just doesn't have the
same positive effects.

01:21:38.730 --> 01:21:41.080
Now, that isn't to say that
if you just weight train

01:21:41.080 --> 01:21:44.000
that you'll be dumb or that
you'll lose your memory

01:21:44.000 --> 01:21:45.370
over time, you might,

01:21:45.370 --> 01:21:48.600
but it is to say that endurance work

01:21:48.600 --> 01:21:50.630
in particular the high intensity

01:21:50.630 --> 01:21:53.120
and long duration work that
I've talked about today.

01:21:53.120 --> 01:21:54.480
The two high intensity protocols

01:21:54.480 --> 01:21:56.950
and the long duration work has been shown

01:21:56.950 --> 01:21:58.550
again and again and again,

01:21:58.550 --> 01:22:00.810
to have positive effects
on brain function.

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:02.580
Not through the addition of new neurons,

01:22:02.580 --> 01:22:04.060
sorry to break it to you,

01:22:04.060 --> 01:22:07.180
but that's not a major
event in the exercised

01:22:07.180 --> 01:22:10.130
or non-exercised human brain,

01:22:10.130 --> 01:22:12.640
for reasons we can talk
about in a future episode.

01:22:12.640 --> 01:22:15.080
But it still has many positive effects

01:22:15.080 --> 01:22:17.850
through the delivery of things like IGF-1,

01:22:17.850 --> 01:22:21.710
but also just through plain
oxygenation of the brain

01:22:21.710 --> 01:22:25.740
and the way it promotes the
development of microvasculature

01:22:25.740 --> 01:22:27.210
to develop, to excuse me,

01:22:27.210 --> 01:22:30.050
to deliver new neurons more nutrients.

01:22:30.050 --> 01:22:33.490
If neurons don't get oxygen
and glucose, they do die.

01:22:33.490 --> 01:22:36.220
Unless there's another
fuel source like ketones

01:22:36.220 --> 01:22:37.720
which can replace the glucose.

01:22:38.900 --> 01:22:41.310
If you don't give oxygen to neurons,

01:22:41.310 --> 01:22:43.240
if you don't deliver enough to them,

01:22:43.240 --> 01:22:44.550
you get what's called ischemia,

01:22:44.550 --> 01:22:46.010
you get little micro strokes.

01:22:46.010 --> 01:22:48.930
So the type of exercise
I'm talking about today

01:22:48.930 --> 01:22:52.040
in generating intense heart rate increases

01:22:52.040 --> 01:22:54.040
provided that safe for you to do.

01:22:54.040 --> 01:22:55.030
Breathing hard,

01:22:55.030 --> 01:22:56.700
that's going to deliver oxygen in blood,

01:22:56.700 --> 01:22:59.310
increase stroke volume of the heart

01:22:59.310 --> 01:23:01.980
and is going to improve brain function

01:23:01.980 --> 01:23:03.930
has been supported by many, many quality

01:23:03.930 --> 01:23:05.600
peer-reviewed studies.

01:23:05.600 --> 01:23:08.850
So that's one form of positive adaptation.

01:23:08.850 --> 01:23:11.180
I also talked about just sort
of performance adaptations.

01:23:11.180 --> 01:23:13.700
How doing high intensity
aerobic conditioning

01:23:13.700 --> 01:23:17.040
of the mile repeats type
training can actually

01:23:17.040 --> 01:23:20.840
improve your ability to do
long bouts of intense work.

01:23:20.840 --> 01:23:23.490
It also seems like it dovetails

01:23:23.490 --> 01:23:26.070
or is compatible with resistance training

01:23:26.070 --> 01:23:28.440
that's aimed towards
strength and hypertrophy.

01:23:28.440 --> 01:23:31.360
Now, in full disclosure
the data seemed to indicate

01:23:31.360 --> 01:23:33.980
that if people just weight
train or train for strength,

01:23:33.980 --> 01:23:37.040
so three reps, rest five minutes,

01:23:37.040 --> 01:23:38.580
three reps of heavyweights, et cetera.

01:23:38.580 --> 01:23:40.990
Yeah, you'll get much
stronger than you would

01:23:40.990 --> 01:23:44.650
if you're doing things like
five repetitions up to 12,

01:23:44.650 --> 01:23:48.380
or 12 to 25 reps and you're
going out for long jogs.

01:23:48.380 --> 01:23:50.890
There's always going to be
a compromise in adaptations,

01:23:50.890 --> 01:23:51.940
unfortunately

01:23:51.940 --> 01:23:53.970
It does seem like you can
do concurrent training

01:23:53.970 --> 01:23:55.810
as I mentioned before,
if you allow anywhere

01:23:55.810 --> 01:23:59.640
from four to six or ideally
24 hours between workouts.

01:23:59.640 --> 01:24:01.240
As I mentioned in the previous episode,

01:24:01.240 --> 01:24:04.350
if you want to know if you
are recovered from a workout?

01:24:04.350 --> 01:24:05.930
A great way to do that is to apply

01:24:05.930 --> 01:24:08.080
the carbon dioxide tolerance test,

01:24:08.080 --> 01:24:09.660
which is four breaths in and out,

01:24:09.660 --> 01:24:11.010
inhale, exhale, inhale exhale,

01:24:11.010 --> 01:24:12.420
inhale, exhale, inhale, exhale,

01:24:12.420 --> 01:24:16.040
then a big inhale and then
a slow controlled exhale.

01:24:16.040 --> 01:24:20.440
If that slow controlled exhale
is 60 seconds or longer,

01:24:20.440 --> 01:24:22.120
it means that your parasympathetic,

01:24:22.120 --> 01:24:24.730
your calming nervous system
is under your control.

01:24:24.730 --> 01:24:26.600
And it's likely, I should say likely

01:24:26.600 --> 01:24:29.360
that systemically your whole
nervous system has recovered

01:24:29.360 --> 01:24:30.820
from whatever it is that you've been doing

01:24:30.820 --> 01:24:33.480
and experiencing in life
including work in relationships.

01:24:33.480 --> 01:24:37.780
If not, you might want to
take a rest day, dare I say.

01:24:37.780 --> 01:24:40.350
Or Costello's on is what?

01:24:40.350 --> 01:24:41.270
He's 10 now.

01:24:41.270 --> 01:24:44.660
I think he's on his 12,000 thrust day.

01:24:44.660 --> 01:24:46.800
Most people need I should say,

01:24:46.800 --> 01:24:49.000
one to two full rest days per week.

01:24:49.000 --> 01:24:49.990
I know there are people going to say

01:24:49.990 --> 01:24:51.420
that's ridiculous and okay,

01:24:51.420 --> 01:24:55.000
maybe you have amazing recovery abilities,

01:24:55.000 --> 01:24:56.500
also depends on training intensity.

01:24:56.500 --> 01:24:58.280
Many people benefit from having one or two

01:24:58.280 --> 01:24:59.890
full rest days per week.

01:24:59.890 --> 01:25:02.310
At least one, some people don't need to.

01:25:02.310 --> 01:25:06.310
But if you are not able
to extend that exhale

01:25:06.310 --> 01:25:10.600
on the carbon dioxide tolerance
test past 60 seconds or so,

01:25:10.600 --> 01:25:14.090
45 seconds, 60 seconds chances are,

01:25:14.090 --> 01:25:16.110
your so-called sympathetic nervous system,

01:25:16.110 --> 01:25:18.290
your stress system is chronically elevated

01:25:18.290 --> 01:25:20.340
and you're not really putting the brake

01:25:20.340 --> 01:25:21.570
on that system enough.

01:25:21.570 --> 01:25:23.210
And that's a subconscious thing.

01:25:23.210 --> 01:25:25.740
There are ways that you
can accelerate recovery,

01:25:25.740 --> 01:25:28.140
but I would encourage you to
listen to the previous episode.

01:25:28.140 --> 01:25:30.890
It's time-stamped for
how to assess recovery.

01:25:30.890 --> 01:25:32.490
So how often to program these things

01:25:32.490 --> 01:25:34.670
will depend on the other
things you're doing.

01:25:34.670 --> 01:25:36.040
I think it's perfectly reasonable

01:25:36.040 --> 01:25:39.380
to do this type of training
with other types of training.

01:25:39.380 --> 01:25:41.960
And I'll talk about a variety
of combinations of those

01:25:41.960 --> 01:25:44.260
toward the end of the episode.

01:25:44.260 --> 01:25:47.210
I do want to talk about
how to deliver more energy

01:25:47.210 --> 01:25:48.680
and oxygen.

01:25:48.680 --> 01:25:51.330
These are tools that are extremely useful,

01:25:51.330 --> 01:25:54.450
I believe and that are
grounded in physiology.

01:25:54.450 --> 01:25:57.040
That three things I'd like to talk about,

01:25:57.040 --> 01:25:59.260
are how to breathe?

01:25:59.260 --> 01:26:03.900
What to do immediately after
training and hydration?

01:26:03.900 --> 01:26:06.450
And I promise I will get
back into programming

01:26:06.450 --> 01:26:07.550
and sort of protocols,

01:26:07.550 --> 01:26:09.666
but these are vitally
important to your ability

01:26:09.666 --> 01:26:13.660
to perform endurance work in particular.

01:26:13.660 --> 01:26:17.440
And they are grounded
in how neurons and blood

01:26:17.440 --> 01:26:19.670
and oxygen and your heart work together.

01:26:19.670 --> 01:26:22.833
So let's first talk about
breathing or respiration.

01:26:23.920 --> 01:26:25.590
We breathe a couple of different ways,

01:26:25.590 --> 01:26:27.410
but let's just remind
ourselves why we breathe?

01:26:27.410 --> 01:26:30.080
We breathe to bring
oxygen into our system,

01:26:30.080 --> 01:26:32.500
and we breathe to get
rid of carbon dioxide.

01:26:32.500 --> 01:26:35.450
And we need both oxygen and carbon dioxide

01:26:35.450 --> 01:26:39.030
in order to utilize fuel and
for our brain and body to work.

01:26:39.030 --> 01:26:42.400
It's not the oxygen is good
and carbon oxide is bad.

01:26:42.400 --> 01:26:45.300
They have to be present
in the appropriate ratios.

01:26:45.300 --> 01:26:49.080
So one thing that is
very clear is our ability

01:26:49.080 --> 01:26:53.020
to deliver oxygen to working
muscles enter our brain

01:26:53.020 --> 01:26:55.710
is going to be important for our ability

01:26:55.710 --> 01:26:57.380
to generate muscular effort

01:26:57.380 --> 01:26:59.530
especially of a kind of what
I was talking about today,

01:26:59.530 --> 01:27:01.290
but also weight training and other forms

01:27:01.290 --> 01:27:03.070
of skill-based effort, et cetera.

01:27:03.070 --> 01:27:04.870
And our ability to think.

01:27:04.870 --> 01:27:06.360
If you're holding your
breath for too long,

01:27:06.360 --> 01:27:07.990
if you're breathing too much,

01:27:07.990 --> 01:27:10.150
if you're what they call
over-breathing or under breathing,

01:27:10.150 --> 01:27:11.140
if you're shallow breathing,

01:27:11.140 --> 01:27:12.070
if you're mouth breathing,

01:27:12.070 --> 01:27:15.300
these are all things that
can really impede mental

01:27:15.300 --> 01:27:16.190
and physical performance.

01:27:16.190 --> 01:27:17.820
So let's make it really simple

01:27:17.820 --> 01:27:21.243
and then I promise to do a
future episode all about recipes.

01:27:21.243 --> 01:27:24.900
There are two main sources
of air for your body

01:27:24.900 --> 01:27:26.320
and it's air coming in through your nose

01:27:26.320 --> 01:27:27.670
and air coming in through your mouth.

01:27:27.670 --> 01:27:30.790
In general nasal breathing is better.

01:27:30.790 --> 01:27:34.580
It scrubs the air of bacteria and viruses.

01:27:34.580 --> 01:27:37.640
You have a microbiome in
your nose that benefits.

01:27:37.640 --> 01:27:38.870
There are a number of reasons,

01:27:38.870 --> 01:27:41.310
it's also just a more efficient
system believe it or not,

01:27:41.310 --> 01:27:42.910
even though it feels like
you can gulp more air

01:27:42.910 --> 01:27:43.910
with your mouth.

01:27:43.910 --> 01:27:47.950
Getting good at nasal breathing is useful.

01:27:47.950 --> 01:27:50.260
A gear - System of the
type that Brian McKenzie

01:27:50.260 --> 01:27:51.370
and colleagues have developed,

01:27:51.370 --> 01:27:52.970
I think is a good way
to conceptualize this.

01:27:52.970 --> 01:27:54.890
If you're doing long duration work,

01:27:54.890 --> 01:27:56.460
try and do it all nasal breathing.

01:27:56.460 --> 01:27:58.070
If you have deviated septum,

01:27:58.070 --> 01:28:01.430
it's probably cause you
don't nasal breathe enough.

01:28:01.430 --> 01:28:06.020
Mouth breathing is something
that many people suffer from.

01:28:06.020 --> 01:28:07.780
You are more prone to infections.

01:28:07.780 --> 01:28:09.560
It's not as efficient, et cetera.

01:28:09.560 --> 01:28:10.910
There is a place for mouth breathing.

01:28:10.910 --> 01:28:15.910
However, it's usually, if you
need to do a strong exhale,

01:28:16.150 --> 01:28:19.580
oftentimes you can discard
more volume through the mouth,

01:28:19.580 --> 01:28:22.000
unless you're very trained
at nasal breathing.

01:28:22.000 --> 01:28:23.850
So if you're doing high
intensity training,

01:28:23.850 --> 01:28:26.900
a good way to conceptualize
this is to exhale

01:28:26.900 --> 01:28:28.790
on the max effort,

01:28:28.790 --> 01:28:32.900
and then to inhale on
the less intense part.

01:28:32.900 --> 01:28:35.890
So that might be as you're
generating the movement,

01:28:35.890 --> 01:28:37.840
you know, you're in the
concentric part of the movement

01:28:37.840 --> 01:28:39.460
you exhale, right?

01:28:39.460 --> 01:28:41.730
Just like on a bat swing
or something like that,

01:28:41.730 --> 01:28:44.120
or fighters and martial artists

01:28:44.120 --> 01:28:46.180
do this differently depending
on how they were trained

01:28:46.180 --> 01:28:47.140
and their different purposes,

01:28:47.140 --> 01:28:49.040
but they kind of like huh or ssh,

01:28:49.040 --> 01:28:51.440
they kind of exhaling during the effort

01:28:51.440 --> 01:28:54.860
and then inhaling on the
portion of the repetition

01:28:54.860 --> 01:28:57.410
that is not the highest effort portion.

01:28:57.410 --> 01:28:59.640
Usually that's the eccentric phase

01:28:59.640 --> 01:29:03.023
of anything involving or
rowing and things of that sort.

01:29:04.140 --> 01:29:07.050
So nasal breathing is great,

01:29:07.050 --> 01:29:09.500
but as you increase the
intensity of your endurance work,

01:29:09.500 --> 01:29:10.790
you will need to incorporate the mouth.

01:29:10.790 --> 01:29:12.080
So a gear system would look something

01:29:12.080 --> 01:29:14.250
like first gear would
be just nasal breathing

01:29:14.250 --> 01:29:16.400
or second gear would also
be just nasal breathing.

01:29:16.400 --> 01:29:18.660
But with more effort, third gear again,

01:29:18.660 --> 01:29:20.460
power speed endurance has
a lot more about this.

01:29:20.460 --> 01:29:21.880
You can go to their website.

01:29:21.880 --> 01:29:24.780
I think it's a very intelligent
way to conceptualize this.

01:29:26.030 --> 01:29:28.210
As you go into more max effort,

01:29:28.210 --> 01:29:30.500
then you're going to third
and fourth and fifth gear.

01:29:30.500 --> 01:29:32.800
And at some point you're not
thinking about nose or mouth.

01:29:32.800 --> 01:29:34.420
You're just trying to
hang on for dear life

01:29:34.420 --> 01:29:36.240
and complete the work safely.

01:29:36.240 --> 01:29:38.200
And that means breathe through

01:29:38.200 --> 01:29:41.730
whatever orifice works for you.

01:29:41.730 --> 01:29:45.250
So that's one aspect, nose versus mouth.

01:29:45.250 --> 01:29:48.710
The other aspect is whether
or not you're using your ribs,

01:29:48.710 --> 01:29:50.650
the intercostal muscles are these muscles

01:29:50.650 --> 01:29:54.990
that the Bruce Lee had these
remarkable intercostal muscles

01:29:54.990 --> 01:29:58.700
that allow you to lift the
rib cage or the diaphragm

01:29:58.700 --> 01:30:00.810
which is a skeletal muscle
that sits below the lungs.

01:30:00.810 --> 01:30:02.130
Just to remind you, when you inhale,

01:30:02.130 --> 01:30:03.200
the diaphragm moves down,

01:30:03.200 --> 01:30:07.190
when you exhale, the diaphragm moves up.

01:30:07.190 --> 01:30:08.580
Okay?

01:30:08.580 --> 01:30:11.370
Here's something that most people don't do

01:30:11.370 --> 01:30:12.920
and would benefit tremendously from.

01:30:12.920 --> 01:30:14.840
And I can say this
because Andy Galpin's lab

01:30:14.840 --> 01:30:18.680
has done work on this
exploring how warming up

01:30:18.680 --> 01:30:21.700
the intercostals and the
nerve to diaphragm pathways

01:30:21.700 --> 01:30:24.130
before any kind of endurance work

01:30:24.130 --> 01:30:26.430
or in the first few
minutes of endurance work

01:30:26.430 --> 01:30:28.880
can allow you to breathe more deeply

01:30:28.880 --> 01:30:31.569
and to deliver more oxygen to the blood,

01:30:31.569 --> 01:30:33.130
[burps] excuse me,

01:30:33.130 --> 01:30:36.810
and to the muscles and to
be able to do more work

01:30:36.810 --> 01:30:37.900
more efficiently.

01:30:37.900 --> 01:30:40.370
So what that involves
is sometimes sitting,

01:30:40.370 --> 01:30:42.420
sometimes standing and
just really concentrating

01:30:42.420 --> 01:30:43.590
on two things.

01:30:43.590 --> 01:30:46.170
We always hear about how we
should diaphragmatic breathe.

01:30:46.170 --> 01:30:49.010
And that means that our belly
moves out when we inhale.

01:30:49.010 --> 01:30:51.370
So [inhales deeply] our stomach expands,

01:30:51.370 --> 01:30:54.220
but also expanding the intercostals

01:30:54.220 --> 01:30:56.700
which means actually raising
the ribs, chest breathing.

01:30:56.700 --> 01:30:58.370
We're all told that in yoga class

01:30:58.370 --> 01:31:00.630
don't breathe with your
chest this, [inhales deeply].

01:31:00.630 --> 01:31:04.510
But actually that is warming
up the intercostal muscles.

01:31:04.510 --> 01:31:07.240
So this is also a great
way to generate adrenaline

01:31:07.240 --> 01:31:09.430
if you do it a little bit intensely.

01:31:09.430 --> 01:31:12.290
So let's say you're feeling
unmotivated to train,

01:31:12.290 --> 01:31:14.850
I don't particularly like
doing endurance training

01:31:14.850 --> 01:31:16.250
until I'm actually doing it.

01:31:17.560 --> 01:31:20.450
So I use and benefit
from having a practice

01:31:20.450 --> 01:31:22.230
where I'll just sit there
and for about three minutes

01:31:22.230 --> 01:31:24.600
I'll just breathe very deeply
trying to raise my chest

01:31:24.600 --> 01:31:26.480
as much as I can for maybe a minute,

01:31:26.480 --> 01:31:29.940
and then contracting my diaphragm

01:31:29.940 --> 01:31:32.410
and expanding my stomach
outward when I inhale.

01:31:32.410 --> 01:31:35.110
By the end of that you're
actually delivering more oxygen

01:31:35.110 --> 01:31:36.033
to your system.

01:31:37.070 --> 01:31:39.660
My lab has looked at this in
a totally different context.

01:31:39.660 --> 01:31:41.400
Andy's lab has looked at it in the context

01:31:41.400 --> 01:31:43.740
of physical performance.

01:31:43.740 --> 01:31:47.330
So warming up the breathing
muscles should make sense,

01:31:47.330 --> 01:31:50.280
given that you now know
that muscles and neurons

01:31:50.280 --> 01:31:53.880
need glucose and they need
oxygen in order to function.

01:31:53.880 --> 01:31:54.930
And so that's a great warmup.

01:31:54.930 --> 01:31:56.740
You can also do this while walking

01:31:56.740 --> 01:31:59.130
or while getting on the
bike and starting to peddle,

01:31:59.130 --> 01:32:01.530
really starting to think about warming up

01:32:01.530 --> 01:32:02.950
the breathing system.

01:32:02.950 --> 01:32:05.100
And then you can decide if
you want to do pure nasal

01:32:05.100 --> 01:32:08.850
or a combination of nasal and
mouth breathing and so on.

01:32:08.850 --> 01:32:10.410
So that's something that
we don't often hear about.

01:32:10.410 --> 01:32:11.720
The other one.

01:32:11.720 --> 01:32:13.840
The other tool, rather that I talked about

01:32:13.840 --> 01:32:15.330
in a previous episode,
I'll just mention again,

01:32:15.330 --> 01:32:17.660
is some people when they
do endurance type work

01:32:17.660 --> 01:32:19.340
they get a stitch in their side.

01:32:19.340 --> 01:32:21.550
They feel like they've got a side cramp.

01:32:21.550 --> 01:32:26.550
Very rarely is it actually
a skeletal muscular cramp,

01:32:26.880 --> 01:32:31.790
it's oftentimes it's a referenced
pain of the phrenic nerve

01:32:31.790 --> 01:32:32.900
that innervates the liver.

01:32:32.900 --> 01:32:34.300
So the phrenic nerve is responsible

01:32:34.300 --> 01:32:36.263
for the movement of the diaphragm.

01:32:37.360 --> 01:32:39.080
It is a very important system,

01:32:39.080 --> 01:32:41.320
but it has a number of
what we call collateral.

01:32:41.320 --> 01:32:43.120
So it branches to other organs.

01:32:43.120 --> 01:32:44.840
Runs over other organs.

01:32:44.840 --> 01:32:46.820
Sometimes when we're breathing shallow

01:32:46.820 --> 01:32:48.570
and we are in physical motion

01:32:48.570 --> 01:32:50.370
and we're engaging in physical effort,

01:32:50.370 --> 01:32:51.750
we'll feel that side stitch.

01:32:51.750 --> 01:32:54.100
And we think, oh, I've got a
cramp or maybe I'm dehydrated

01:32:54.100 --> 01:32:57.010
or maybe I need to run
with my hands over my head,

01:32:57.010 --> 01:32:57.970
excuse me.

01:32:57.970 --> 01:33:00.520
Typically, you can
relieve that side cramp,

01:33:00.520 --> 01:33:01.710
which isn't a cramp at all.

01:33:01.710 --> 01:33:04.620
That side stitch by doing
the double inhale, exhale,

01:33:04.620 --> 01:33:06.710
really breathing deeply. [inhales deeply]

01:33:06.710 --> 01:33:09.830
And then sneaking a
little bit more air in.

01:33:09.830 --> 01:33:12.350
That's a double kind of firing

01:33:12.350 --> 01:33:14.480
or what we call volume of action potential

01:33:14.480 --> 01:33:18.070
sent from the phrenic
nerve to the diaphragm,

01:33:18.070 --> 01:33:20.600
which will also activate that collateral,

01:33:20.600 --> 01:33:22.520
that branch literally of the nerve

01:33:22.520 --> 01:33:23.970
that innervates the liver.

01:33:23.970 --> 01:33:26.320
And then when you exhale,

01:33:26.320 --> 01:33:28.180
you offload a bunch of carbon dioxide,

01:33:28.180 --> 01:33:31.790
but if you repeat that a few times often,

01:33:31.790 --> 01:33:34.010
in fact, for me every time
but often what'll happen

01:33:34.010 --> 01:33:36.330
is that side stitch will
just naturally disappear.

01:33:36.330 --> 01:33:38.070
Just means you're not breathing properly.

01:33:38.070 --> 01:33:40.840
You're the phrenic
nerve is firing in a way

01:33:40.840 --> 01:33:43.490
that's kind of aggravating
that referenced pain.

01:33:43.490 --> 01:33:45.950
There's nothing kind of voodoo
or mysterious about this.

01:33:45.950 --> 01:33:48.780
It just has to do with the
way that the different nerves

01:33:48.780 --> 01:33:50.260
travel in the body.

01:33:50.260 --> 01:33:52.080
So as you set out on your run

01:33:52.080 --> 01:33:54.880
or maybe you're going to do
some muscular endurance work

01:33:54.880 --> 01:33:56.780
or high intensity work,

01:33:56.780 --> 01:33:58.620
warming up the intercostals,

01:33:58.620 --> 01:34:01.160
warming up the diaphragm is good.

01:34:01.160 --> 01:34:03.890
And there are exercises and there is work

01:34:03.890 --> 01:34:06.380
that you can do to
strengthen the intercostals

01:34:06.380 --> 01:34:08.480
and to strengthen the diaphragm

01:34:08.480 --> 01:34:10.240
during bouts of this kind of effort.

01:34:10.240 --> 01:34:12.060
And I would say that one of the ways

01:34:12.060 --> 01:34:14.737
that you can do that best
is by really focusing

01:34:14.737 --> 01:34:19.210
on getting the maximum
diaphragmatic expansion

01:34:19.210 --> 01:34:20.700
and chest lifting.

01:34:20.700 --> 01:34:21.960
What we're all told now not to do,

01:34:21.960 --> 01:34:23.840
you know, don't chest
breathe, belly breathe.

01:34:23.840 --> 01:34:25.640
The intercostals are there for a reason

01:34:25.640 --> 01:34:27.317
and they are perfectly
good at filling your lungs.

01:34:27.317 --> 01:34:29.120
And they work best when they collaborate

01:34:29.120 --> 01:34:30.240
with your diaphragm.

01:34:30.240 --> 01:34:32.780
But when you are starting to fatigue

01:34:32.780 --> 01:34:35.100
to start to really inhale deeply

01:34:35.100 --> 01:34:36.780
and try and really expand those to deliver

01:34:36.780 --> 01:34:39.310
more oxygen to your system.

01:34:39.310 --> 01:34:41.230
While we're talking about
delivering more oxygen

01:34:41.230 --> 01:34:42.270
to your system,

01:34:42.270 --> 01:34:44.070
I want to share with you a useful tool

01:34:44.070 --> 01:34:47.070
that will now make total sense
mechanistically why it works,

01:34:47.070 --> 01:34:51.070
which is oftentimes when
we are on a long run

01:34:51.070 --> 01:34:53.453
or in long duration bouts of effort.

01:34:54.500 --> 01:34:57.060
We will hit the so-called wall, right?

01:34:57.060 --> 01:34:57.893
We will bonk.

01:34:57.893 --> 01:34:59.210
I think they used to call it,

01:34:59.210 --> 01:35:00.760
do they still call it that Costello?

01:35:00.760 --> 01:35:02.410
He's asleep.

01:35:02.410 --> 01:35:03.243
We bonk.

01:35:03.243 --> 01:35:05.840
Where we just we think
no, we can't continue.

01:35:05.840 --> 01:35:08.750
It's a curious thing as to
whether or not that's neural

01:35:08.750 --> 01:35:10.760
or whether or not it's fuel-based,

01:35:10.760 --> 01:35:12.940
there's certainly going
to be a psychological

01:35:12.940 --> 01:35:14.490
or motivational component.

01:35:14.490 --> 01:35:17.830
But one way that you can
reveal this kind of extra gear,

01:35:17.830 --> 01:35:20.210
the capacity to push on is
by understanding the way

01:35:20.210 --> 01:35:24.470
that different muscle fibers
use energy differently.

01:35:24.470 --> 01:35:26.917
Remember the fast-twitch
phosphocreatine system

01:35:26.917 --> 01:35:28.910
and the slow twitch
system that relies mainly

01:35:28.910 --> 01:35:30.470
on lipids and glucose.

01:35:30.470 --> 01:35:32.400
Okay, well, even if you
don't remember all that,

01:35:32.400 --> 01:35:34.017
if you've been running
steadily for a long time

01:35:34.017 --> 01:35:35.460
and you're starting to fatigue

01:35:35.460 --> 01:35:37.283
and you feel like it's time to quit.

01:35:38.520 --> 01:35:41.740
You may have not tapped into
an alternative fuel source.

01:35:41.740 --> 01:35:43.600
One thing that you can
do is you can actually

01:35:43.600 --> 01:35:45.200
increase your speed.

01:35:45.200 --> 01:35:48.870
This is also true of work
where you're doing repetitions

01:35:48.870 --> 01:35:49.940
with kettlebells or something.

01:35:49.940 --> 01:35:51.700
You can start to increase your speed.

01:35:51.700 --> 01:35:55.780
So run faster, pedal faster,
row faster, swim faster

01:35:55.780 --> 01:35:57.670
not all out sprint.

01:35:57.670 --> 01:36:00.400
But in doing that, you're
shifting the the muscles

01:36:00.400 --> 01:36:03.800
and the nerves over towards
utilizing a separate fuel source

01:36:03.800 --> 01:36:05.740
or a distinct fuel source.

01:36:05.740 --> 01:36:07.200
Maybe the phosphocreatine system

01:36:07.200 --> 01:36:10.460
if it's a quick about
of intense acceleration,

01:36:10.460 --> 01:36:14.040
or maybe it's a combination
of lipids and carbohydrates

01:36:14.040 --> 01:36:17.110
in your system that weren't
available to you prior.

01:36:17.110 --> 01:36:18.530
Now, of course, if you completely deplete

01:36:18.530 --> 01:36:19.363
your liver glycogen,

01:36:19.363 --> 01:36:21.400
you completely deplete everything,

01:36:21.400 --> 01:36:24.200
you're only going to be
running on stored fuel and fats

01:36:24.200 --> 01:36:27.280
and eventually you'll
start metabolizing protein,

01:36:27.280 --> 01:36:28.820
muscles themselves.

01:36:28.820 --> 01:36:33.590
But this is a kind of a
unique way to realize that,

01:36:33.590 --> 01:36:36.580
oh, you weren't out of energy at all,

01:36:36.580 --> 01:36:39.020
you're just over-relying
on one fuel source.

01:36:39.020 --> 01:36:40.530
And this is the reason why,

01:36:40.530 --> 01:36:43.340
especially elite athletes
are starting to both rely

01:36:43.340 --> 01:36:44.173
on carbohydrates.

01:36:44.173 --> 01:36:45.710
So they're doing the whole carb depletion

01:36:45.710 --> 01:36:46.780
then carb loading thing.

01:36:46.780 --> 01:36:48.350
They're loading up their
liver and their muscles

01:36:48.350 --> 01:36:50.994
with plenty of glycogen
by eating pastas and rice

01:36:50.994 --> 01:36:51.879
and stuff before races.

01:36:51.879 --> 01:36:56.879
But they are also ingesting
ketones during races,

01:36:56.930 --> 01:36:58.470
during long bouts of effort,

01:36:58.470 --> 01:37:00.700
because ketones can be
a quick form of energy.

01:37:00.700 --> 01:37:02.640
There's no reason why
you can't use ketones

01:37:02.640 --> 01:37:06.110
if they are taking exogenous
ketones and carbohydrate.

01:37:06.110 --> 01:37:08.730
And in combination, remember
the body is accustomed

01:37:08.730 --> 01:37:12.360
to using multiple fuel sources,
fatty acids, carbohydrates

01:37:12.360 --> 01:37:13.193
all these things.

01:37:13.193 --> 01:37:15.780
It's only in the, you
know, of internet age

01:37:15.780 --> 01:37:17.170
that we think in terms of,

01:37:17.170 --> 01:37:20.750
oh, well you're either keto
or you're burning sugar,

01:37:20.750 --> 01:37:24.730
or you're fat adapted
or key or fat fasting

01:37:24.730 --> 01:37:26.992
or fast fasting or fat fatting.

01:37:26.992 --> 01:37:28.800
Costello woke up when I said fat fatting.

01:37:28.800 --> 01:37:30.310
I'm not talking about you Costello.

01:37:30.310 --> 01:37:32.190
So the point is that your body

01:37:32.190 --> 01:37:33.780
is used to using multiple fuel sources.

01:37:33.780 --> 01:37:35.700
So if you're kind of hitting that wall,

01:37:35.700 --> 01:37:38.620
sometimes accelerating can
actually allow you to tap

01:37:38.620 --> 01:37:41.080
into a new fuel source or
combination of fuel sources

01:37:41.080 --> 01:37:43.780
just based on the way
that muscles use fuel.

01:37:43.780 --> 01:37:45.530
So that's another tool.

01:37:45.530 --> 01:37:47.200
The other thing that's really
important to think about

01:37:47.200 --> 01:37:50.550
in terms of endurance
type work is hydration.

01:37:50.550 --> 01:37:53.130
And I think hydration is
important for all forms

01:37:53.130 --> 01:37:56.190
of physical work and
exercise not just endurance.

01:37:56.190 --> 01:38:00.560
The deal with hydration
is that we've been taught

01:38:00.560 --> 01:38:01.990
about hydration all wrong.

01:38:01.990 --> 01:38:05.050
But let's remember what neurons work on?

01:38:05.050 --> 01:38:06.930
What do they use in order to fire?

01:38:06.930 --> 01:38:10.670
Well, they certainly need water, right?

01:38:10.670 --> 01:38:12.910
We need water in our system, I should say.

01:38:12.910 --> 01:38:14.700
But remember they use electrolytes,

01:38:14.700 --> 01:38:17.060
sodium and potassium to
generate those action potentials

01:38:17.060 --> 01:38:18.870
to actually get neurons to contract,

01:38:18.870 --> 01:38:20.800
to be able, excuse me, muscles to contract

01:38:20.800 --> 01:38:23.813
and for our brain to function
and to be able to think.

01:38:26.030 --> 01:38:28.190
Typically, we're going to lose anywhere

01:38:28.190 --> 01:38:32.210
from one to five pounds of
water per hour of exercise.

01:38:32.210 --> 01:38:33.700
And that's going to vary tremendously,

01:38:33.700 --> 01:38:35.500
it's going to vary on weather,

01:38:35.500 --> 01:38:37.410
it's going to vary on intensity,

01:38:37.410 --> 01:38:39.020
probably more like five pounds,

01:38:39.020 --> 01:38:43.100
if it's hot day and you're
exercising very intensely.

01:38:43.100 --> 01:38:46.230
So about one to five pounds per hour.

01:38:46.230 --> 01:38:48.690
Now, you know how much you weigh.

01:38:48.690 --> 01:38:52.620
So if you think about
your weight in pounds,

01:38:52.620 --> 01:38:57.620
once you lose about one to 4%
of your body weight in water,

01:38:58.080 --> 01:39:01.870
you're going to experience
about a 20 to 30% reduction

01:39:01.870 --> 01:39:02.890
in work capacity.

01:39:02.890 --> 01:39:07.340
In your ability to generate
effort of any kind,

01:39:07.340 --> 01:39:09.850
strength, endurance, et cetera.

01:39:09.850 --> 01:39:12.450
You are also going to
experience a significant drop

01:39:12.450 --> 01:39:15.580
in your ability to think and
perform mental operations.

01:39:15.580 --> 01:39:16.813
So hydration is key.

01:39:16.813 --> 01:39:18.540
Now, many people have been told,

01:39:18.540 --> 01:39:21.810
well, if you urinate
and your urine is clear,

01:39:21.810 --> 01:39:23.520
well, then you're hydrated enough.

01:39:23.520 --> 01:39:24.720
Sometimes that's true.

01:39:24.720 --> 01:39:26.330
Sometimes that's not true.

01:39:26.330 --> 01:39:29.700
Also and this isn't a
topic I enjoyed discussing,

01:39:29.700 --> 01:39:32.709
but a urine is a biological phenomenon.

01:39:32.709 --> 01:39:36.770
It's actually filtered
blood every once in a while

01:39:36.770 --> 01:39:38.460
and if there's a kid and
it's a family friend,

01:39:38.460 --> 01:39:39.777
I'll say, "Did you know that your pee

01:39:39.777 --> 01:39:41.097
"is actually filtered blood?"

01:39:41.097 --> 01:39:42.710
And they usually kind of go wide-eyed.

01:39:42.710 --> 01:39:44.040
But then they go, "Oh,
that's kind of cool."

01:39:44.040 --> 01:39:45.720
Like kids have this natural curiosity

01:39:45.720 --> 01:39:47.610
about blood and pee and stuff.

01:39:47.610 --> 01:39:51.190
That's not contaminated by
our preconceived notions

01:39:51.190 --> 01:39:53.570
of those things being gross,

01:39:53.570 --> 01:39:56.400
because you're in being filtered blood

01:39:56.400 --> 01:39:58.470
can give you some indication as to whether

01:39:58.470 --> 01:40:01.050
or not you're hydrated enough or not.

01:40:01.050 --> 01:40:03.570
And in order to really assess that,

01:40:03.570 --> 01:40:06.430
it's not going to be sufficient to urinate

01:40:06.430 --> 01:40:08.500
into another volume of water and assess

01:40:08.500 --> 01:40:10.270
whether or not your urine
is very dark or very light.

01:40:10.270 --> 01:40:12.530
It actually requires
urinating into a small volume

01:40:12.530 --> 01:40:15.050
and saying, well, is it
darker or lighter than before?

01:40:15.050 --> 01:40:18.103
It's not something you really
want to do most places.

01:40:19.520 --> 01:40:22.290
The etiquette of most
gyms and environments

01:40:22.290 --> 01:40:23.810
is not suitable for that.

01:40:23.810 --> 01:40:26.700
But one of the things that you can just do

01:40:26.700 --> 01:40:27.533
is you configure,

01:40:27.533 --> 01:40:30.860
well, I'm going to lose one to
five pounds of water per hour.

01:40:30.860 --> 01:40:35.740
You can show up to exercise
reasonably hydrated

01:40:35.740 --> 01:40:36.910
with electrolytes.

01:40:36.910 --> 01:40:39.310
So potassium, sodium and
magnesium are really key.

01:40:39.310 --> 01:40:42.210
Yes, it's true, you can die
from drinking too much water

01:40:42.210 --> 01:40:44.500
in particular because it forces you,

01:40:44.500 --> 01:40:45.710
if you drink too much water,

01:40:45.710 --> 01:40:47.420
you excrete too many electrolytes

01:40:47.420 --> 01:40:49.080
and your brain will shut off.

01:40:49.080 --> 01:40:51.540
You'll actually your heart
will stop functioning properly.

01:40:51.540 --> 01:40:54.800
So you don't want to over
consume to the extreme either,

01:40:54.800 --> 01:40:57.280
but there are a number of equations

01:40:57.280 --> 01:40:59.590
that go into figuring out
how much water you need

01:40:59.590 --> 01:41:02.040
based on how intense
your training, et cetera,

01:41:02.040 --> 01:41:02.990
body size, et cetera.

01:41:02.990 --> 01:41:05.550
Just remember you burn,
you lose, excuse me

01:41:05.550 --> 01:41:08.580
about one to five pounds
of water per hour,

01:41:08.580 --> 01:41:09.413
depending on how hot it is,

01:41:09.413 --> 01:41:11.340
and how intensely you're exercising.

01:41:11.340 --> 01:41:14.320
Once your body weight drops by one to 4%.

01:41:14.320 --> 01:41:15.600
So you can just figure,

01:41:15.600 --> 01:41:17.810
well, if you lose five pounds per hour,

01:41:17.810 --> 01:41:20.120
you exercise for two hours.

01:41:20.120 --> 01:41:21.520
Let's say you're about 200 pounds,

01:41:21.520 --> 01:41:22.930
that's about 10%.

01:41:22.930 --> 01:41:23.763
Okay?

01:41:23.763 --> 01:41:27.490
Well, you want to replace that
before you very quickly or not.

01:41:27.490 --> 01:41:29.140
You want to replace that all along

01:41:29.140 --> 01:41:30.570
before you start experiencing

01:41:30.570 --> 01:41:33.890
this massive 20 to 30% reduction
in work capacity of muscles

01:41:33.890 --> 01:41:34.723
and the brain.

01:41:34.723 --> 01:41:36.080
A simple formula.

01:41:36.080 --> 01:41:38.120
What I call it, the Galpin equation.

01:41:38.120 --> 01:41:41.260
Hereafter, referred to
as the Galpin equation

01:41:41.260 --> 01:41:44.590
is a formula that gets you
close to the exact amount

01:41:44.590 --> 01:41:47.510
that you would want that Dr.
Andy Galpin came up with,

01:41:47.510 --> 01:41:50.130
which is your body weight in pounds

01:41:50.130 --> 01:41:52.200
divided by the number 30.

01:41:52.200 --> 01:41:54.220
And that is how many
ounces you should drink

01:41:54.220 --> 01:41:57.310
for every 15 minutes of exercise.

01:41:57.310 --> 01:41:59.620
So once again, the Galpin equation.

01:41:59.620 --> 01:42:03.490
Your body weight in pounds divided by 30.

01:42:03.490 --> 01:42:08.490
That's the amount of fluid
to drink in ounces, right?

01:42:09.690 --> 01:42:12.420
Every 15 minutes of exercise.

01:42:12.420 --> 01:42:13.880
Now, if you are sweating a lot,

01:42:13.880 --> 01:42:15.060
you may need more.

01:42:15.060 --> 01:42:15.893
Okay?

01:42:15.893 --> 01:42:17.800
If you're already very well hydrated,

01:42:17.800 --> 01:42:19.120
you may need less,

01:42:19.120 --> 01:42:21.999
but that's a good rule of thumb to begin

01:42:21.999 --> 01:42:24.810
and to start to understand
the relationship

01:42:24.810 --> 01:42:27.490
between hydration and performance.

01:42:27.490 --> 01:42:32.250
There is a phenomenon in
which gastric emptying,

01:42:32.250 --> 01:42:34.450
the ability to move stuff out of your gut,

01:42:34.450 --> 01:42:37.440
including water and
electrolytes out of your gut

01:42:37.440 --> 01:42:39.900
and into the bloodstream and for delivery

01:42:39.900 --> 01:42:41.920
to the tissues of your body for effort

01:42:41.920 --> 01:42:45.380
is hindered when you get
above 70% of your VO2 max.

01:42:45.380 --> 01:42:48.470
In other words when you're
doing high intensity training

01:42:48.470 --> 01:42:51.280
sometimes people experience
that ingesting water

01:42:52.160 --> 01:42:54.940
during intense training is difficult.

01:42:54.940 --> 01:42:57.940
It is something that can
be actually trained up.

01:42:57.940 --> 01:43:00.840
It's a matter of learning to kind of relax

01:43:00.840 --> 01:43:02.730
your abdominal muscles.

01:43:02.730 --> 01:43:05.680
And there's some other
aspects of adaptation

01:43:05.680 --> 01:43:09.380
that will allow you to drink
during higher intensity work.

01:43:09.380 --> 01:43:14.027
As Galpin says, don't
try and ingest fluids

01:43:14.027 --> 01:43:16.950
when you're working out or competing

01:43:16.950 --> 01:43:18.840
at higher than 70% of VO2 max

01:43:18.840 --> 01:43:19.860
if you've never done it before.

01:43:19.860 --> 01:43:21.930
You want to train up this capacity.

01:43:21.930 --> 01:43:24.380
People can learn how to
consume fluids during a race

01:43:24.380 --> 01:43:27.060
or consume fluids during bouts of exercise

01:43:27.060 --> 01:43:28.790
that are very intense.

01:43:28.790 --> 01:43:30.440
And a lot of people don't want to do that,

01:43:30.440 --> 01:43:32.750
'cause they don't want to have
to stop to urinate, et cetera.

01:43:32.750 --> 01:43:35.500
But given the crucial role of hydration

01:43:35.500 --> 01:43:38.640
for muscular performance
and for brain performance,

01:43:38.640 --> 01:43:40.660
it seems that if you're
going to be doing a lot

01:43:40.660 --> 01:43:42.790
of high intensity interval training

01:43:42.790 --> 01:43:45.170
of the various concepts talked about today

01:43:45.170 --> 01:43:46.960
or high intensity training of any kind

01:43:46.960 --> 01:43:49.040
that hydration is key and learning

01:43:49.040 --> 01:43:51.180
or in other words, getting
your system to adapt

01:43:51.180 --> 01:43:53.630
to ingesting fluids in the
middle of these workouts

01:43:53.630 --> 01:43:55.660
is something that seems beneficial.

01:43:55.660 --> 01:43:58.290
At least to me, in terms of the trade off

01:43:58.290 --> 01:44:01.350
between being dehydrated
and the somewhat discomfort

01:44:01.350 --> 01:44:02.470
of maybe drinking some fluids.

01:44:02.470 --> 01:44:04.670
So you sip small amounts
of fluid initially

01:44:04.670 --> 01:44:06.680
and then you're able to
take bigger and bigger gulps

01:44:06.680 --> 01:44:07.513
as time goes on.

01:44:07.513 --> 01:44:11.710
And pretty soon you're able
to drink mid-set or be,

01:44:11.710 --> 01:44:13.110
excuse me, not mid-set.

01:44:13.110 --> 01:44:15.120
Please don't do that between
sets and your workout

01:44:15.120 --> 01:44:17.410
or while you're still breathing
hard after a mile repeat

01:44:17.410 --> 01:44:20.010
or something of that sort
without much disruption

01:44:20.010 --> 01:44:22.040
or any at all to your performance.

01:44:22.040 --> 01:44:25.260
Last episode we talked
about how to assess recovery

01:44:25.260 --> 01:44:28.220
and things that you might want
to do to improve recovery,

01:44:28.220 --> 01:44:29.760
how exposure to ice baths

01:44:29.760 --> 01:44:31.620
and cold showers can reduce inflammation

01:44:31.620 --> 01:44:33.550
which can be great for recovery,

01:44:33.550 --> 01:44:36.700
but can inhibit some of the adaptations

01:44:36.700 --> 01:44:37.970
for strength and hypertrophy.

01:44:37.970 --> 01:44:40.540
Because inflammation isn't good or bad.

01:44:40.540 --> 01:44:43.940
Inflammation isn't like a
nice person or a mean person.

01:44:43.940 --> 01:44:44.880
It's both.

01:44:44.880 --> 01:44:48.600
It's a great thing for
stimulating adaptations,

01:44:48.600 --> 01:44:50.330
but you don't want it around too long.

01:44:50.330 --> 01:44:52.460
And so we suggested that
you not do ice baths

01:44:52.460 --> 01:44:55.660
within probably six hours of any training

01:44:55.660 --> 01:44:58.770
where the goal was hypertrophy
or strength training.

01:44:58.770 --> 01:45:00.900
There is some evidence
that getting yourself

01:45:00.900 --> 01:45:04.930
into an ice bath or cold
shower after endurance training

01:45:04.930 --> 01:45:08.847
can actually improve the
mitochondrial aspects

01:45:09.990 --> 01:45:12.810
of endurance exercise that
you can get improvements

01:45:12.810 --> 01:45:14.800
in mitochondrial density,

01:45:14.800 --> 01:45:18.760
and you can get improvements
in mitochondrial respiration

01:45:18.760 --> 01:45:20.050
by doing that afterwards

01:45:20.050 --> 01:45:22.120
and that it can facilitate recovery.

01:45:22.120 --> 01:45:24.820
That's still a bit of
a controversial area.

01:45:24.820 --> 01:45:26.400
I do think that what I mentioned earlier

01:45:26.400 --> 01:45:27.760
that waiting at least six hours

01:45:27.760 --> 01:45:30.630
and probably more like
24 hours between workouts

01:45:30.630 --> 01:45:31.463
is a good idea.

01:45:31.463 --> 01:45:34.340
That getting at least one
full day of rest each week,

01:45:34.340 --> 01:45:35.610
for some people that'll be two.

01:45:35.610 --> 01:45:37.210
I have to say I'm one of these people

01:45:37.210 --> 01:45:40.100
that after two days of
absolutely no exercise,

01:45:40.100 --> 01:45:43.400
I do perform better
consistently across all aspects

01:45:43.400 --> 01:45:46.790
of physical performance and
mentally I feel better as well

01:45:46.790 --> 01:45:49.160
even though I load to take those days off

01:45:49.160 --> 01:45:51.200
unless I'm really exhausted

01:45:51.200 --> 01:45:53.230
it does seem to help my training.

01:45:53.230 --> 01:45:55.240
Some people can train seven
days a week and they're fine.

01:45:55.240 --> 01:45:57.860
I think it just is there's a
lot of individual variation.

01:45:57.860 --> 01:45:59.810
You want to work on sleep
and maximizing sleep

01:45:59.810 --> 01:46:01.963
for recovery nutrition,
of course, as well.

01:46:02.840 --> 01:46:06.400
I talked about sleep in
the first four episodes

01:46:06.400 --> 01:46:07.233
of the podcast.

01:46:07.233 --> 01:46:08.770
If you have trouble with sleep

01:46:08.770 --> 01:46:11.130
definitely check out those episodes.

01:46:11.130 --> 01:46:14.750
It's very clear and a
number of sports teams

01:46:14.750 --> 01:46:16.840
even some folks that I work with,

01:46:16.840 --> 01:46:20.110
and Andy Galpin and others
are starting to incorporate

01:46:20.110 --> 01:46:22.630
a what's called a
parasympathetic down-regulation

01:46:22.630 --> 01:46:25.540
after training of any kind as
a way to accelerate recovery

01:46:25.540 --> 01:46:27.460
and enable you to do more work.

01:46:27.460 --> 01:46:29.540
In other words, get
back to work out sooner.

01:46:29.540 --> 01:46:30.610
What is parasympathetic downregulation?

01:46:30.610 --> 01:46:33.630
It means finishing your training

01:46:33.630 --> 01:46:35.880
and instead of just hopping on the phone

01:46:35.880 --> 01:46:37.400
or hopping into your car

01:46:37.400 --> 01:46:40.670
and heading off to take
five minutes minimum,

01:46:40.670 --> 01:46:42.790
maybe ideally more like 10 or 20,

01:46:42.790 --> 01:46:45.220
but for sake of time five minutes minimum

01:46:45.220 --> 01:46:50.220
and doing just some slow pure
nasal long exhale breathing

01:46:50.310 --> 01:46:53.110
or lying down and just kind of zoning out.

01:46:53.110 --> 01:46:55.850
That it seems can accelerate recovery

01:46:55.850 --> 01:46:59.040
and allow you to get back
into other types of work,

01:46:59.040 --> 01:47:01.260
mental work or physical work more quickly

01:47:01.260 --> 01:47:05.420
which makes total sense because
remember your nervous system

01:47:05.420 --> 01:47:08.580
and recovery and work
is a local phenomenon

01:47:08.580 --> 01:47:09.870
which muscles were you using.

01:47:09.870 --> 01:47:11.430
You know, were you using your glutes,

01:47:11.430 --> 01:47:13.110
your hams and your back

01:47:13.110 --> 01:47:14.730
or were using your shoulders, et cetera.

01:47:14.730 --> 01:47:16.510
But it's also a systemic thing.

01:47:16.510 --> 01:47:17.650
It's also about those neurons

01:47:17.650 --> 01:47:19.457
in the locus coeruleus that
are releasing epinephrine

01:47:19.457 --> 01:47:22.250
and you want to quiet all
that down after training.

01:47:22.250 --> 01:47:23.990
You want to really just zone out.

01:47:23.990 --> 01:47:26.720
Think Costello, channel
your inner Costello,

01:47:26.720 --> 01:47:29.770
and just mellow out for five to 20 minutes

01:47:29.770 --> 01:47:31.880
and then move into the rest of your day.

01:47:31.880 --> 01:47:33.670
Five minutes should be manageable.

01:47:33.670 --> 01:47:35.550
Even if it's just sitting in
the car with your eyes closed

01:47:35.550 --> 01:47:37.580
doing that downregulation breathing.

01:47:37.580 --> 01:47:39.750
I think you'll see big benefits in terms

01:47:39.750 --> 01:47:42.590
of allowing yourself to come back sooner,

01:47:42.590 --> 01:47:44.490
do more work over time

01:47:44.490 --> 01:47:47.340
and just perform and
feel better generally,

01:47:47.340 --> 01:47:49.150
as well as be able to
think about other things

01:47:49.150 --> 01:47:51.800
besides the just how
much the previous workout

01:47:51.800 --> 01:47:52.700
kind of beat you up.

01:47:53.700 --> 01:47:56.030
A couple more things I think
are going to be useful.

01:47:56.030 --> 01:47:57.810
And I do want to just pack these in,

01:47:57.810 --> 01:48:00.600
'cause we are closing out the
month on physical performance.

01:48:00.600 --> 01:48:04.970
And that's about
programming and about pacing

01:48:04.970 --> 01:48:08.320
and the kind of mental
aspects of endurance.

01:48:08.320 --> 01:48:12.710
So let's start with pacing and
mental aspects of endurance.

01:48:12.710 --> 01:48:17.330
I learned from a friend and
colleague here at the podcast

01:48:17.330 --> 01:48:18.163
that...

01:48:18.163 --> 01:48:20.650
Who's very active in triathlon
and marathon and other...

01:48:20.650 --> 01:48:22.310
Knows a lot about that whole world

01:48:22.310 --> 01:48:24.140
and the competitive landscape there,

01:48:24.140 --> 01:48:28.170
that pacing and literally physical pacers

01:48:28.170 --> 01:48:29.510
have a laser on the ground

01:48:29.510 --> 01:48:33.680
or visualizing or having a
pace car or a pace runner

01:48:33.680 --> 01:48:37.150
in front is actually not
allowed in many competitions.

01:48:37.150 --> 01:48:41.130
And if those are present
doesn't allow the race times

01:48:41.130 --> 01:48:45.670
to qualify as legitimate
record holding times.

01:48:45.670 --> 01:48:47.420
And that's very interesting to me

01:48:47.420 --> 01:48:51.790
because what we know is
that the visual system

01:48:51.790 --> 01:48:54.950
has this capacity to switch
back and forth between

01:48:54.950 --> 01:48:56.230
what we call panoramic vision

01:48:56.230 --> 01:48:57.980
where we're not really
focused on anything.

01:48:57.980 --> 01:48:59.800
Things are just flowing past us

01:48:59.800 --> 01:49:02.330
or our eyes are just kind of zoned out.

01:49:02.330 --> 01:49:04.640
So I can do this right now
and you won't be able to tell

01:49:04.640 --> 01:49:06.230
but I'm looking at the
corners of the room.

01:49:06.230 --> 01:49:07.850
I see Costello down there on the floor.

01:49:07.850 --> 01:49:10.360
I see my podcast team here

01:49:10.360 --> 01:49:12.630
and I can also see the microphone.

01:49:12.630 --> 01:49:14.870
I can see myself in this environment.

01:49:14.870 --> 01:49:16.150
That's panoramic vision.

01:49:16.150 --> 01:49:18.310
Whereas if I draw my eyes to one location

01:49:18.310 --> 01:49:20.050
like right there in the
center of the camera,

01:49:20.050 --> 01:49:21.630
it's what's called a
vergence eye movement.

01:49:21.630 --> 01:49:23.860
So I'm contracting my visual window.

01:49:23.860 --> 01:49:27.170
The contraction of the visual
window when that's done,

01:49:27.170 --> 01:49:28.380
is the same thing that would happen

01:49:28.380 --> 01:49:31.350
if I was tracking say a
pace car or a pace runner

01:49:31.350 --> 01:49:33.320
or a laser on the ground.

01:49:33.320 --> 01:49:35.330
The mere bringing our eyes together

01:49:35.330 --> 01:49:39.010
to what we call a vergence
point has the impact

01:49:39.010 --> 01:49:41.790
of triggering the activation
of neural circuits

01:49:41.790 --> 01:49:43.080
in the thalamus.

01:49:43.080 --> 01:49:44.550
Things like zona incerta,

01:49:44.550 --> 01:49:46.120
if you really want to
know what their names are,

01:49:46.120 --> 01:49:46.953
of these brain areas.

01:49:46.953 --> 01:49:49.700
As well as in the brainstem that activate

01:49:49.700 --> 01:49:51.070
the so-called alertness system,

01:49:51.070 --> 01:49:52.430
things like locus coeruleus,

01:49:52.430 --> 01:49:55.430
whereas panoramic vision tends to bring us

01:49:55.430 --> 01:49:56.990
into states of relaxation.

01:49:56.990 --> 01:49:59.760
You can actually leverage
us during your runs.

01:49:59.760 --> 01:50:02.100
Let's say you're out for a long run

01:50:02.100 --> 01:50:04.010
or you're swimming or you're cycling.

01:50:04.010 --> 01:50:06.572
This is probably easiest
to imagine out of the water

01:50:06.572 --> 01:50:08.450
before I do in the water as well.

01:50:08.450 --> 01:50:10.640
If you focus your attention on a landmark

01:50:10.640 --> 01:50:12.370
that you're going to run to,

01:50:12.370 --> 01:50:14.070
you'll find that it's much easier

01:50:14.070 --> 01:50:17.320
than if you don't actually
have a set milestone

01:50:17.320 --> 01:50:19.340
or landmark that you're going to run to.

01:50:19.340 --> 01:50:21.640
However, if you were to
continue that repeatedly

01:50:21.640 --> 01:50:24.270
just going milestone after
milestone, after milestone,

01:50:24.270 --> 01:50:26.540
you would feel more mentally fatigued

01:50:26.540 --> 01:50:27.700
and you would actually be able

01:50:27.700 --> 01:50:30.580
to degenerate less work overall.

01:50:30.580 --> 01:50:35.160
One thing that can be useful
is focusing on a milestone,

01:50:35.160 --> 01:50:37.190
running to that milestone or biking

01:50:37.190 --> 01:50:38.700
whatever it is the activity happens to be,

01:50:38.700 --> 01:50:40.700
and then dilating your field of view

01:50:40.700 --> 01:50:43.640
to relax the system and
then continuing again.

01:50:43.640 --> 01:50:46.190
So it's this kind of active contraction

01:50:46.190 --> 01:50:48.550
or of the visual window and then dilation

01:50:48.550 --> 01:50:49.700
of the visual window.

01:50:49.700 --> 01:50:51.840
Contraction of the visual
window allows you to generate

01:50:51.840 --> 01:50:53.560
more effort but there's
a cost to doing that,

01:50:53.560 --> 01:50:55.010
because neurons consume energy.

01:50:55.010 --> 01:50:56.800
And now you know how they do that.

01:50:56.800 --> 01:50:59.194
Whereas dilation allows you to essentially

01:50:59.194 --> 01:51:01.290
be more efficient, right?

01:51:01.290 --> 01:51:06.290
Now, pacing is not allowed or
having a pacer, a visual pacer

01:51:06.370 --> 01:51:08.740
because it does allow
you to access systems

01:51:08.740 --> 01:51:13.010
in the brain and body that
allow you to create more energy,

01:51:13.010 --> 01:51:13.843
more effort.

01:51:13.843 --> 01:51:14.940
And so I find it interesting

01:51:14.940 --> 01:51:17.270
that I think in a kind
of subconscious genius

01:51:17.270 --> 01:51:20.420
the race officials and
the governing bodies

01:51:20.420 --> 01:51:21.820
of these races have said,

01:51:21.820 --> 01:51:24.400
okay, sure, having a pacer
there or someone in front,

01:51:24.400 --> 01:51:25.390
you can draft off of them,

01:51:25.390 --> 01:51:28.480
there's actually a kind
of a aerodynamic effect

01:51:28.480 --> 01:51:29.410
of having someone in front of you

01:51:29.410 --> 01:51:33.490
that makes it easier to run in
the wake of their airstream.

01:51:33.490 --> 01:51:34.430
So to speak.

01:51:34.430 --> 01:51:36.290
Same as true in cycling.

01:51:36.290 --> 01:51:37.920
This is why the cycling teams are so good

01:51:37.920 --> 01:51:39.120
at maneuvering and packs.

01:51:39.120 --> 01:51:42.320
In very specific ways, you
can go faster with less effort

01:51:42.320 --> 01:51:45.230
if you're drafting as it's
called behind somebody.

01:51:45.230 --> 01:51:48.700
But as well where you place
your vision will allow you

01:51:48.700 --> 01:51:50.280
to generate more effort.

01:51:50.280 --> 01:51:52.100
And so it's interesting
that they've taken out

01:51:52.100 --> 01:51:55.260
this kind of, if you will,

01:51:55.260 --> 01:51:57.430
performance enhancing tool.

01:51:57.430 --> 01:51:59.730
I imagine and I have to imagine

01:51:59.730 --> 01:52:01.020
it's the appropriate word here.

01:52:01.020 --> 01:52:06.020
That good runners, good
cyclists have the ability

01:52:06.210 --> 01:52:10.420
to create a kind of
pacer in their minds eye.

01:52:10.420 --> 01:52:12.740
I have to imagine that they're
not just completely allowing

01:52:12.740 --> 01:52:13.810
their attention to drift,

01:52:13.810 --> 01:52:16.390
although they do that when they
want to be in highly efficient

01:52:16.390 --> 01:52:18.640
mode generating effort
without having to tax

01:52:18.640 --> 01:52:19.770
their mental capacity.

01:52:19.770 --> 01:52:22.730
And remember mental
capacity is neural energy

01:52:22.730 --> 01:52:25.480
and consumes glucose energy
that they could devote

01:52:25.480 --> 01:52:26.990
to the functioning of their body.

01:52:26.990 --> 01:52:30.640
But that when needed that they
can focus their energy in,

01:52:30.640 --> 01:52:34.270
and actually kind of chase a
mental pacer or pick milestone.

01:52:34.270 --> 01:52:37.410
So this is a mental game
that you can play as well.

01:52:37.410 --> 01:52:39.790
It's a little bit hard to do
in the context of weightlifting

01:52:39.790 --> 01:52:40.623
in the gym.

01:52:40.623 --> 01:52:42.700
It's more of a moving
through space kind of thing,

01:52:42.700 --> 01:52:46.010
but some people do this by
counting reps, et cetera.

01:52:46.010 --> 01:52:49.820
I think it's especially
suitable for endurance type

01:52:49.820 --> 01:52:52.180
of exercise, especially done outside.

01:52:52.180 --> 01:52:54.010
One of the reasons I hate
running on a treadmill

01:52:54.010 --> 01:52:56.020
is it just feels like it's never ending.

01:52:56.020 --> 01:52:58.090
And I've never tried one
of these Peloton things.

01:52:58.090 --> 01:52:59.450
I try and avoid looking at screens

01:52:59.450 --> 01:53:01.210
as much as I possibly can.

01:53:01.210 --> 01:53:03.430
But if you try this next
time you're out for a run

01:53:03.430 --> 01:53:04.263
or a swim.

01:53:04.263 --> 01:53:05.720
What you'll find is
that you have a capacity

01:53:05.720 --> 01:53:09.750
to engage a system of higher energy output

01:53:09.750 --> 01:53:11.680
when you focus your eyes
on a particular location,

01:53:11.680 --> 01:53:13.310
but you want to use that judiciously

01:53:13.310 --> 01:53:15.000
because your goal of course,

01:53:15.000 --> 01:53:17.660
is to become efficient at
moving through space over time

01:53:17.660 --> 01:53:19.890
and not taxing your brain
and body to the point

01:53:19.890 --> 01:53:22.530
where you arrive at the end of that,

01:53:22.530 --> 01:53:25.670
unless it's race day just
completely tapped out.

01:53:25.670 --> 01:53:29.130
So that's a kind of
interesting aspect of running.

01:53:29.130 --> 01:53:32.020
If you're a fan of running, which I am,

01:53:32.020 --> 01:53:34.740
and you get the chance to look
at any of the documentaries

01:53:34.740 --> 01:53:37.310
or docu-drama has made
about, [clears throat]

01:53:37.310 --> 01:53:39.280
excuse me, about Steve Prefontaine.

01:53:39.280 --> 01:53:42.130
It was clear that he was mostly
in a battle with himself,

01:53:42.130 --> 01:53:44.440
but that he was also a highly
competitive individual.

01:53:44.440 --> 01:53:46.270
And you'll see this in some of his races.

01:53:46.270 --> 01:53:48.620
I do encourage you to look
some of those up on YouTube

01:53:48.620 --> 01:53:49.760
or see the docudramas.

01:53:49.760 --> 01:53:50.720
They're quite good.

01:53:50.720 --> 01:53:52.690
Where he ran the...

01:53:53.640 --> 01:53:55.480
Essentially was 12 laps on a track.

01:53:55.480 --> 01:53:56.615
It's essentially the five.

01:53:56.615 --> 01:53:57.850
It is the 5,000 meter race

01:53:57.850 --> 01:53:59.700
where essentially three miles.

01:53:59.700 --> 01:54:01.940
And he essentially tried
to sprint the whole thing

01:54:01.940 --> 01:54:02.790
which is ridiculous.

01:54:02.790 --> 01:54:04.050
Actually knowing what you know today

01:54:04.050 --> 01:54:05.930
you'll realize that Steve Prefontaine

01:54:05.930 --> 01:54:08.520
basically was pulling
from strength, speed,

01:54:08.520 --> 01:54:10.710
power, muscular, endurance,

01:54:10.710 --> 01:54:12.540
long duration effort, high intensity,

01:54:12.540 --> 01:54:14.240
aerobic, anaerobic as he sort of tried

01:54:14.240 --> 01:54:16.080
to maximize every fuel system.

01:54:16.080 --> 01:54:18.700
And you'll see that in
the races that he runs,

01:54:18.700 --> 01:54:22.740
but that when runners are
nearing the final laps,

01:54:22.740 --> 01:54:24.300
the so-called bell lap of a race,

01:54:24.300 --> 01:54:26.200
they'll often look to one another to see

01:54:26.200 --> 01:54:28.980
where somebody is obviously
to assess their progress

01:54:28.980 --> 01:54:30.470
and how close somebody is?

01:54:30.470 --> 01:54:32.910
But when somebody gets past,

01:54:32.910 --> 01:54:37.300
oftentimes you'll see someone
access this mysterious kick,

01:54:37.300 --> 01:54:40.280
this ability to tap into
some additional gear

01:54:40.280 --> 01:54:43.090
that allows them to run forward or faster

01:54:43.090 --> 01:54:45.190
when they themselves actually thought

01:54:45.190 --> 01:54:46.700
that they were maxed out.

01:54:46.700 --> 01:54:48.840
So someone could be running
for the finish line.

01:54:48.840 --> 01:54:49.990
They're convinced they're going to win.

01:54:49.990 --> 01:54:51.160
They're going max effort

01:54:51.160 --> 01:54:52.500
or at least they perceive max effort.

01:54:52.500 --> 01:54:53.550
Someone passes them.

01:54:53.550 --> 01:54:56.250
And all of a sudden
max effort has changed,

01:54:56.250 --> 01:54:57.970
because of that visual target

01:54:57.970 --> 01:55:00.540
they are able to access higher levels

01:55:00.540 --> 01:55:03.060
of speed and output and
effort and performance.

01:55:03.060 --> 01:55:05.260
They don't always catch
up to that person and win,

01:55:05.260 --> 01:55:09.660
but having a target a
milestone is a powerful way

01:55:09.660 --> 01:55:13.010
that we can generate more
force and energy in anything.

01:55:13.010 --> 01:55:15.690
And the visual system
is the way that we bring

01:55:15.690 --> 01:55:18.550
those milestones into our brain,

01:55:18.550 --> 01:55:20.510
which then brings about epinephrin,

01:55:20.510 --> 01:55:22.340
which brings about neural firing,

01:55:22.340 --> 01:55:24.760
which allows us to
access whatever resources

01:55:24.760 --> 01:55:26.550
happen to be available to us.

01:55:26.550 --> 01:55:30.640
So I find this fascinating
because people often wonder,

01:55:30.640 --> 01:55:32.370
like where does the kick come from?

01:55:32.370 --> 01:55:35.380
Where is this kind of gift
of an additional gear?

01:55:35.380 --> 01:55:37.010
Where is that deeper resource?

01:55:37.010 --> 01:55:39.290
And we often express it and talk about it,

01:55:39.290 --> 01:55:42.680
in kind of psychological
terms like heart or willpower

01:55:42.680 --> 01:55:45.560
or that something kind of
got transplanted into us

01:55:45.560 --> 01:55:46.750
or descended into us.

01:55:46.750 --> 01:55:50.310
And not to remove any of the
spiritual aspects of sport

01:55:50.310 --> 01:55:54.540
or running or effort of
the human, you know, heart.

01:55:54.540 --> 01:55:57.270
But it's very clear
that the nervous system

01:55:57.270 --> 01:55:59.580
when it has a specific visual target

01:55:59.580 --> 01:56:02.770
can generate the sorts of intense effort

01:56:02.770 --> 01:56:04.170
that it couldn't otherwise.

01:56:04.170 --> 01:56:05.810
And it sometimes even comes as a surprise

01:56:05.810 --> 01:56:08.090
to the person generating the effort.

01:56:08.090 --> 01:56:10.400
I promised that I would
talk about programming,

01:56:10.400 --> 01:56:13.968
meaning when and how many times a week

01:56:13.968 --> 01:56:16.607
to do the various workouts
related to endurance

01:56:16.607 --> 01:56:19.460
and how to merge those with
other types of exercise

01:56:19.460 --> 01:56:21.680
